WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.495 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.495 --> 00:00:04.491 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.491 --> 00:00:16.480 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.800 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: But yeah, we are very
glad to have John Hull with us

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:21.300 align:middle line:90%
here.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:22.720 align:middle line:90%
So John, please.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:24.320 align:middle line:84%
JOHN HULL: Well,
thanks very much.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:27.360 align:middle line:90%
It's a real pleasure to be here.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:30.140 align:middle line:84%
Let me just say a little
bit by way of introduction.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:33.680 align:middle line:84%
I probably got a similar
background to many of you.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:38.560 align:middle line:84%
Math was always my best
subject at high school.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:42.786 align:middle line:90%
That was in the UK.

00:00:42.786 --> 00:00:48.200 align:middle line:84%
I, not surprisingly, ended
up doing a math undergraduate

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:49.220 align:middle line:90%
at Cambridge.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:51.960 align:middle line:90%


00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:57.040 align:middle line:84%
And after graduating,
I decided that I wanted

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:00.500 align:middle line:90%
to apply my math in business.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:04.140 align:middle line:84%
So I did a master's in
operational research, which

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.090 align:middle line:84%
was a fairly new
thing in those days.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.860 align:middle line:90%


00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:16.300 align:middle line:84%
And then an amusing
little factoid,

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:19.780 align:middle line:84%
I worked for two years after
graduating with my master's--

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:22.720 align:middle line:84%
I worked for two years
for a shoe retailer.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:26.320 align:middle line:84%
Everybody bursts out
laughing when they hear this.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:30.140 align:middle line:84%
Actually, it was a large
shoe retailer in the UK.

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:32.300 align:middle line:90%
It had 2,000 outlets.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.740 align:middle line:84%
And actually, it was a
pretty interesting job.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:37.620 align:middle line:84%
I mean, it was
all about deciding

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:43.160 align:middle line:84%
which outlets should stock which
shoes in which sizes and so on,

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:46.300 align:middle line:84%
and then which outlets
should be closed

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:49.300 align:middle line:84%
and where we should look for new
outlets and that sort of thing.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:52.500 align:middle line:84%
So there's lots of
interesting stuff.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:57.680 align:middle line:84%
But after two years
with this company,

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:01.110 align:middle line:84%
I moved back into
the academic world.

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:05.960 align:middle line:90%


00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:07.780 align:middle line:84%
I won't bore you
with all the details.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:13.600 align:middle line:84%
But I wound up I got
my PhD and ended up

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:17.320 align:middle line:84%
teaching here at University
of Toronto in what we

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:19.960 align:middle line:84%
call the Joseph L. Rotman
School of Management.

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:25.600 align:middle line:84%
And I've been at University of
Toronto for about 30 years now.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:32.520 align:middle line:84%
Probably I'm best
known for my work

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:38.080 align:middle line:84%
on derivatives, which I
understand you guys have had

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.980 align:middle line:90%
quite a bit of instruction in.

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:45.200 align:middle line:84%
And most of my 30 years has
been spent doing research

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:48.580 align:middle line:84%
in derivatives, but I
guess it was about seven,

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:57.501 align:middle line:84%
eight years ago, I began to
see that machine learning

00:02:57.501 --> 00:03:01.790 align:middle line:84%
and AI were becoming really
important to our students.

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:07.190 align:middle line:90%
And so I switched my focus.

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:09.990 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, all of my
teaching is now in the machine

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:12.510 align:middle line:90%
learning area.

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:14.080 align:middle line:90%
And pretty much every--

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:16.975 align:middle line:90%


00:03:16.975 --> 00:03:20.110 align:middle line:90%
just let me advance slides.

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:26.870 align:middle line:84%
Pretty much every course
that we offer at Rotman

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:31.870 align:middle line:84%
actually has either a
compulsory course or an elective

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:33.310 align:middle line:90%
in machine learning.

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:36.150 align:middle line:84%
So it's not just me that
teaches machine learning.

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:41.140 align:middle line:84%
I've now got quite a few
colleagues who do so as well.

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:43.950 align:middle line:90%


00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:49.030 align:middle line:84%
I wrote a book, which is
now in its third edition,

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:50.830 align:middle line:90%
Machine Learning in Business--

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:53.550 align:middle line:84%
An Introduction to the
World of Data Science.

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:56.730 align:middle line:84%
And you can find
out more information

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:59.010 align:middle line:90%
on the book from my website.

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:02.530 align:middle line:84%
But I'm not saying you
should buy the book now.

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:09.090 align:middle line:84%
In fact, the reverse is true
because the fourth edition

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:12.850 align:middle line:84%
will be coming out in
probably about two months.

00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:16.529 align:middle line:84%
The fourth edition is going to
be with three co-authors who

00:04:16.529 --> 00:04:21.130 align:middle line:84%
are actually the other people
who teach machine learning here

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:22.410 align:middle line:90%
at Rotman.

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:26.730 align:middle line:84%
So the fourth edition
is going to deal with--

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:29.530 align:middle line:90%


00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:34.490 align:middle line:84%
I mean, third edition came
out in I think it was 2021.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:38.130 align:middle line:84%
And since then, of course,
we've had a lot of things

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:43.290 align:middle line:84%
happen in natural language
processing, large language

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:45.230 align:middle line:90%
models, that sort of thing.

00:04:45.230 --> 00:04:50.410 align:middle line:84%
So I wanted to bring
the book up to date.

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:52.560 align:middle line:84%
And my colleagues have
helped me do that.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:55.230 align:middle line:90%


00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:58.030 align:middle line:84%
Really, today's presentation
is in three parts.

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:03.190 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to talk about the
nature of machine learning.

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:06.030 align:middle line:84%
Then I'm going to move on
to talk about applications

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:08.390 align:middle line:84%
of neural networks,
and finally, what

00:05:08.390 --> 00:05:11.150 align:middle line:84%
I've been working on quite a
lot over the last little while,

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:13.990 align:middle line:84%
applications of
reinforcement learning.

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:15.190 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:18.230 align:middle line:84%
I don't know whether there's
any easy way you can interrupt

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:21.030 align:middle line:84%
me to ask questions,
but I'm happy to be

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:24.670 align:middle line:84%
interrupted with any questions
you have as we go along.

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:29.870 align:middle line:84%
Is it possible for them to
interrupt me with questions?

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:31.850 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: If they speak
up with a question,

00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:34.230 align:middle line:90%
you'll hear them speak up.

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:35.935 align:middle line:90%
JOHN HULL: I will hear them?

00:05:35.935 --> 00:05:37.310 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Yes,
I think you will.

00:05:37.310 --> 00:05:37.930 align:middle line:90%
JOHN HULL: Good, good.

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:38.430 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:40.670 align:middle line:84%
No, I don't mind being
interrupted at all.

00:05:40.670 --> 00:05:42.462 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:05:42.462 --> 00:05:45.970 align:middle line:90%
So next slide, please.

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:49.350 align:middle line:90%


00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:51.350 align:middle line:90%
So what is machine learning?

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:53.610 align:middle line:90%
It's a branch of AI.

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:55.970 align:middle line:84%
And the idea underlying
machine learning

00:05:55.970 --> 00:06:00.610 align:middle line:84%
is that we give a computer
access to lots of data

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:04.290 align:middle line:84%
and just let it learn, learn
about the relationships

00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:08.970 align:middle line:84%
between variables
and make predictions.

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:11.210 align:middle line:84%
Some of the techniques
of machine learning

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:14.610 align:middle line:90%
date back to quite a long way.

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:17.370 align:middle line:84%
The key thing that's
different today

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:20.010 align:middle line:84%
is that computers
are really fast.

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:23.490 align:middle line:84%
So we had all these techniques
for doing what we call machine

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:25.730 align:middle line:90%
learning quite some time ago.

00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:28.290 align:middle line:84%
But really computers
weren't fast enough

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:30.450 align:middle line:90%
to make them a practical tool.

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:35.050 align:middle line:84%
We're now reaching the stage
where all of these ideas

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:37.850 align:middle line:84%
suddenly become
practical tools because

00:06:37.850 --> 00:06:40.050 align:middle line:90%
of the speed of the machines.

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:41.210 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:45.810 align:middle line:84%
I think it's important
to distinguish

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:49.850 align:middle line:84%
between machine learning and
all the automation that's

00:06:49.850 --> 00:06:51.490 align:middle line:90%
gone on before it.

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:54.350 align:middle line:84%
I mean, computers have
been used for many years

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:56.830 align:middle line:84%
to automate many
business decisions--

00:06:56.830 --> 00:07:01.230 align:middle line:84%
payroll, sending out
invoices, and so on.

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:04.990 align:middle line:84%
This is what we could call the
third Industrial Revolution.

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:06.870 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:09.790 align:middle line:84%
And machine learning is central
to the fourth Industrial

00:07:09.790 --> 00:07:11.470 align:middle line:90%
Revolution.

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:13.270 align:middle line:84%
If you want a bit
of background here,

00:07:13.270 --> 00:07:17.630 align:middle line:84%
the first Industrial Revolution
was steam and water power,

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:22.950 align:middle line:90%
which was about 1760 to 1840.

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:26.750 align:middle line:84%
The second Industrial Revolution
was electricity and mass

00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:27.690 align:middle line:90%
production.

00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:31.470 align:middle line:90%


00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:32.410 align:middle line:90%
This is not me.

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:35.830 align:middle line:84%
This is what's generally
considered to be the case.

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:39.550 align:middle line:90%
And this was 1840 to 1920.

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:45.150 align:middle line:84%
Then basically, computers
and digitization

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:46.850 align:middle line:84%
were the third
Industrial Revolution.

00:07:46.850 --> 00:07:49.110 align:middle line:90%
And we've really had it.

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:52.370 align:middle line:84%
We now know how to use
computers to automate things

00:07:52.370 --> 00:07:55.690 align:middle line:90%
that human beings do.

00:07:55.690 --> 00:08:01.930 align:middle line:84%
And let's say we can call that
computers and digitization.

00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:08.410 align:middle line:84%
We could say that went from
maybe 1950 to the year 2000.

00:08:08.410 --> 00:08:14.290 align:middle line:84%
And then we're now living
through the fourth Industrial

00:08:14.290 --> 00:08:15.770 align:middle line:90%
Revolution.

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:18.570 align:middle line:84%
And we'll be living through
it for quite some time, which

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:24.970 align:middle line:84%
is really AI, machine learning
and everything we're going

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:27.630 align:middle line:90%
to be talking about today.

00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:28.130 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:29.230 align:middle line:90%
Next slide, please.

00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:33.130 align:middle line:90%


00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:39.409 align:middle line:84%
So if you want to
go back to slide 6,

00:08:39.409 --> 00:08:45.650 align:middle line:84%
if you want to distinguish
between the third

00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:50.240 align:middle line:84%
and the fourth
Industrial Revolution,

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:54.280 align:middle line:84%
one example I use is
with loan applications.

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:57.160 align:middle line:84%
I mean, let's suppose we've
got loan officers in a company.

00:08:57.160 --> 00:08:59.880 align:middle line:84%
And there are certain
rules they use

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:02.880 align:middle line:84%
to decide whether a loan
should be accepted or not.

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:10.113 align:middle line:84%
And those rules have
been worked out.

00:09:10.113 --> 00:09:11.780 align:middle line:84%
Then we could automate
their activities.

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:14.440 align:middle line:84%
And that would just be the
third Industrial Revolution.

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:18.120 align:middle line:84%
If we didn't know the
rules, we could actually

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:21.520 align:middle line:84%
use machine learning
to determine the rules.

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:24.200 align:middle line:84%
We'd collect lots
and lots of data

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:29.000 align:middle line:84%
on loans that came before the
loan officers and the decisions

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:30.360 align:middle line:90%
that were made.

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:34.920 align:middle line:84%
And it would be an application
of machine learning.

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:36.580 align:middle line:84%
We could go one step
further, though,

00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:40.040 align:middle line:84%
and we could also
say, well, let's try

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:41.400 align:middle line:90%
and improve on the rules.

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:45.480 align:middle line:84%
So then we could collect
lots of data on decisions

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:48.260 align:middle line:84%
that were made by
the loan officers

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:50.900 align:middle line:84%
and how those
decisions worked out,

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:56.540 align:middle line:84%
and try and improve the rules
for accepting or rejecting

00:09:56.540 --> 00:09:58.900 align:middle line:90%
loans.

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:02.540 align:middle line:84%
So the first bullet point
here is the third Industrial

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:06.460 align:middle line:84%
Revolution, digitization,
fairly straightforward, and then

00:10:06.460 --> 00:10:07.980 align:middle line:84%
the last two would
be applications

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:09.480 align:middle line:90%
of machine learning.

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:09.980 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:10.550 align:middle line:90%
Next slide.

00:10:10.550 --> 00:10:13.820 align:middle line:90%


00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:18.220 align:middle line:84%
So I'm sure you've all
been trained in statistics.

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:22.220 align:middle line:84%
And we all know that what we're
supposed to do in statistics

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:29.620 align:middle line:84%
is sit down, comfortable
chair, not look at any data,

00:10:29.620 --> 00:10:31.940 align:middle line:90%
and develop a hypothesis.

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:35.040 align:middle line:84%
Then we collect data
and test the hypothesis.

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:40.620 align:middle line:84%
That's the standard way of
doing things in statistics.

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:43.020 align:middle line:90%
Machine learning is different.

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:46.340 align:middle line:84%
What we do, we don't develop any
hypotheses in machine learning.

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:49.360 align:middle line:84%
We go out and we
collect the data.

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:50.860 align:middle line:90%
And we try and explain the data.

00:10:50.860 --> 00:10:53.860 align:middle line:90%
We try different models.

00:10:53.860 --> 00:10:58.360 align:middle line:84%
And we find patterns in
the data or try and develop

00:10:58.360 --> 00:11:00.460 align:middle line:84%
predictive tool or
something like that.

00:11:00.460 --> 00:11:03.920 align:middle line:84%
But the key difference
between machine learning

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:10.960 align:middle line:84%
and the traditional
Statistical approach

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:16.960 align:middle line:84%
is that, in machine learning,
we collect the data first

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:18.760 align:middle line:84%
where, in statistics,
you're not supposed

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:20.220 align:middle line:90%
to collect the data first.

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:22.920 align:middle line:84%
You're supposed to develop the
hypothesis before you've even

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:24.940 align:middle line:90%
looked at the data.

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:25.440 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:28.880 align:middle line:90%


00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:29.450 align:middle line:90%
Next slide.

00:11:29.450 --> 00:11:33.320 align:middle line:90%


00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:39.960 align:middle line:84%
So there's various types of
machine learning algorithms.

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:42.160 align:middle line:84%
Unsupervised learning,
which is basically

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:48.340 align:middle line:84%
just looking for patterns in the
data or clusters in the data.

00:11:48.340 --> 00:11:49.940 align:middle line:84%
Supervised learning,
which is where

00:11:49.940 --> 00:11:51.900 align:middle line:84%
we're trying to
predict something

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:54.780 align:middle line:84%
or classify the
data in some way.

00:11:54.780 --> 00:11:57.660 align:middle line:84%
And then reinforcement
learning, which

00:11:57.660 --> 00:12:00.340 align:middle line:84%
is multi-stage decision
making, which we'll be

00:12:00.340 --> 00:12:03.740 align:middle line:90%
talking a bit about later on.

00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:08.480 align:middle line:84%
And there's other ways of
subdividing the algorithms.

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:18.380 align:middle line:84%
But this is one quick
classification, if you like.

00:12:18.380 --> 00:12:22.380 align:middle line:84%
Machine learning has
its own terminology.

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:25.720 align:middle line:84%
And actually, when I first got
into machine learning, as I say,

00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:28.300 align:middle line:84%
it was something like
seven or eight years ago, I

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:30.900 align:middle line:84%
was pretty confused
because the terminology is

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:32.900 align:middle line:90%
different from statistics.

00:12:32.900 --> 00:12:37.670 align:middle line:90%


00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:41.660 align:middle line:84%
In statistics, for example, we
talk about dependent variables,

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:44.960 align:middle line:84%
independent
variables, and so on,

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:52.680 align:middle line:84%
whereas, in machine learning, we
talk about features and targets.

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:54.640 align:middle line:84%
Targets, as the name
implies, is the thing

00:12:54.640 --> 00:12:56.520 align:middle line:84%
you're trying to
predict, perhaps.

00:12:56.520 --> 00:12:59.360 align:middle line:84%
And features are the
independent variables

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:03.820 align:middle line:84%
you're trying to predict
from, things like labels,

00:13:03.820 --> 00:13:06.240 align:middle line:90%
activation functions, and so on.

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:11.480 align:middle line:84%
In fact, when I wrote my book,
which I showed you earlier, I

00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:15.880 align:middle line:84%
have a whole section, I think
it's about 10 pages long,

00:13:15.880 --> 00:13:25.040 align:middle line:84%
showing all the terminology
which machine learning uses,

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:28.280 align:middle line:84%
some of which is quite
different from the terminology

00:13:28.280 --> 00:13:31.250 align:middle line:84%
that statistics uses
and other branches.

00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:36.320 align:middle line:90%


00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:42.160 align:middle line:84%
It's one of the things that just
takes a bit of getting used to.

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:47.190 align:middle line:84%
And this terminology,
unsupervised learning,

00:13:47.190 --> 00:13:49.550 align:middle line:84%
supervised learning,
for example.

00:13:49.550 --> 00:13:51.150 align:middle line:84%
I mean where does
that come from?

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:57.070 align:middle line:84%
Unsupervised learning, well,
with unsupervised learning,

00:13:57.070 --> 00:14:00.510 align:middle line:84%
you're just looking for
patterns in the data.

00:14:00.510 --> 00:14:04.270 align:middle line:84%
So you could say
don't really need to--

00:14:04.270 --> 00:14:06.110 align:middle line:84%
you don't really
need any supervision

00:14:06.110 --> 00:14:09.140 align:middle line:84%
because all you're doing is
just looking for patterns.

00:14:09.140 --> 00:14:11.830 align:middle line:90%


00:14:11.830 --> 00:14:16.870 align:middle line:84%
And so you just-- all you need
to do is to throw the data

00:14:16.870 --> 00:14:20.970 align:middle line:84%
at the software and the software
will find patterns for you,

00:14:20.970 --> 00:14:24.030 align:middle line:84%
whereas supervised learning,
you've got to do a bit

00:14:24.030 --> 00:14:27.003 align:middle line:84%
of supervision because
you've got to tell--

00:14:27.003 --> 00:14:28.670 align:middle line:84%
you've got to tell
the computer what you

00:14:28.670 --> 00:14:30.710 align:middle line:90%
want it to do with the data.

00:14:30.710 --> 00:14:33.670 align:middle line:84%
You want it to predict
something, classify data

00:14:33.670 --> 00:14:36.990 align:middle line:90%
in some way, and so on.

00:14:36.990 --> 00:14:39.470 align:middle line:90%
So OK.

00:14:39.470 --> 00:14:40.110 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:14:40.110 --> 00:14:43.330 align:middle line:90%
Next slide.

00:14:43.330 --> 00:14:46.850 align:middle line:84%
So just one slide on
unsupervised learning then.

00:14:46.850 --> 00:14:53.730 align:middle line:84%
So in a typical application,
what you're asking,

00:14:53.730 --> 00:14:58.650 align:middle line:84%
you give the
software data and you

00:14:58.650 --> 00:15:01.050 align:middle line:84%
ask it to divide the
data into clusters.

00:15:01.050 --> 00:15:03.890 align:middle line:84%
So the observations
in the cluster

00:15:03.890 --> 00:15:06.530 align:middle line:90%
have similar feature values.

00:15:06.530 --> 00:15:07.070 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:15:07.070 --> 00:15:09.450 align:middle line:84%
And so a typical
application would

00:15:09.450 --> 00:15:13.810 align:middle line:84%
be a company wants to
understand its customers better.

00:15:13.810 --> 00:15:17.290 align:middle line:84%
So it collects attributes
of its customers,

00:15:17.290 --> 00:15:23.690 align:middle line:84%
maybe 10 different
attributes that it collects,

00:15:23.690 --> 00:15:25.770 align:middle line:84%
like how much the
customer spends

00:15:25.770 --> 00:15:30.850 align:middle line:84%
every year, how many different
products the customer buys,

00:15:30.850 --> 00:15:35.970 align:middle line:84%
where the customer is
located, and so on and so on.

00:15:35.970 --> 00:15:43.510 align:middle line:84%
And basically, you
give the software

00:15:43.510 --> 00:15:47.470 align:middle line:84%
a list of your customers
with those attributes.

00:15:47.470 --> 00:15:52.170 align:middle line:84%
And it will classify
them or cluster them.

00:15:52.170 --> 00:15:56.383 align:middle line:84%
So you say, oh yeah, we've got
customers that look like this.

00:15:56.383 --> 00:15:58.050 align:middle line:84%
We've got customers
that look like this,

00:15:58.050 --> 00:16:01.310 align:middle line:90%
we've got customers, and so on.

00:16:01.310 --> 00:16:06.270 align:middle line:84%
And I'm told that this is
actually quite a powerful tool

00:16:06.270 --> 00:16:11.990 align:middle line:84%
because sometimes you get to
find clusters of customers

00:16:11.990 --> 00:16:14.310 align:middle line:84%
that you never really
thought of as being

00:16:14.310 --> 00:16:18.750 align:middle line:84%
a cluster of your customers
before but this tool has

00:16:18.750 --> 00:16:25.590 align:middle line:84%
actually enabled you
to find the clusters.

00:16:25.590 --> 00:16:26.250 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:16:26.250 --> 00:16:28.990 align:middle line:90%
Next slide.

00:16:28.990 --> 00:16:32.150 align:middle line:84%
So supervised learning
is where you're not just

00:16:32.150 --> 00:16:35.270 align:middle line:84%
trying to understand the
data and create clusters

00:16:35.270 --> 00:16:36.830 align:middle line:90%
from your data.

00:16:36.830 --> 00:16:41.450 align:middle line:84%
You're actually trying to
predict something, typically.

00:16:41.450 --> 00:16:47.210 align:middle line:84%
And what you do is you
divide your data into three

00:16:47.210 --> 00:16:50.390 align:middle line:84%
sets, training set,
a validation set,

00:16:50.390 --> 00:16:54.930 align:middle line:84%
and a test set because,
remember, what we're doing here

00:16:54.930 --> 00:16:55.890 align:middle line:90%
is not--

00:16:55.890 --> 00:16:57.470 align:middle line:90%
we haven't got a hypothesis.

00:16:57.470 --> 00:16:59.850 align:middle line:90%
We've just got a lot of data.

00:16:59.850 --> 00:17:03.970 align:middle line:84%
And so, typically, you
put about 60% of your data

00:17:03.970 --> 00:17:08.450 align:middle line:84%
into the training set, 20% into
the validation set, and 20%

00:17:08.450 --> 00:17:09.553 align:middle line:90%
into the test set.

00:17:09.553 --> 00:17:11.470 align:middle line:84%
And hopefully, you've
got quite a lot of data.

00:17:11.470 --> 00:17:16.470 align:middle line:90%


00:17:16.470 --> 00:17:22.530 align:middle line:84%
So you develop different
models using the training set.

00:17:22.530 --> 00:17:25.450 align:middle line:90%
So you try out different models.

00:17:25.450 --> 00:17:27.970 align:middle line:84%
And we'll see some examples
of this in a moment.

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:36.610 align:middle line:84%
And you compare those
models using validation set.

00:17:36.610 --> 00:17:40.750 align:middle line:84%
Say, well, this model seems to
do better than this other model

00:17:40.750 --> 00:17:43.320 align:middle line:90%
when tested on--

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:46.990 align:middle line:84%
the parameters of the
model are determined

00:17:46.990 --> 00:17:50.070 align:middle line:90%
using the training set.

00:17:50.070 --> 00:17:53.390 align:middle line:84%
And then you can see
how well the models work

00:17:53.390 --> 00:17:59.590 align:middle line:84%
on the validation
set and decide which

00:17:59.590 --> 00:18:02.860 align:middle line:84%
seems to be the best model
for explaining the data.

00:18:02.860 --> 00:18:06.310 align:middle line:90%


00:18:06.310 --> 00:18:13.830 align:middle line:84%
Typically, you can find a really
complex model, sometimes anyway,

00:18:13.830 --> 00:18:17.750 align:middle line:84%
that will explain the
data in the training set

00:18:17.750 --> 00:18:22.550 align:middle line:84%
but then it does not do
well on the validation set.

00:18:22.550 --> 00:18:25.990 align:middle line:90%
So the model can be too complex.

00:18:25.990 --> 00:18:26.690 align:middle line:90%
So we'll see.

00:18:26.690 --> 00:18:30.190 align:middle line:84%
So you want to,
typically, you want

00:18:30.190 --> 00:18:33.030 align:middle line:84%
to-- a rule of thumb is
you want to increase model

00:18:33.030 --> 00:18:37.210 align:middle line:84%
complexity until the model
starts to perform worse

00:18:37.210 --> 00:18:38.550 align:middle line:90%
on the validation set.

00:18:38.550 --> 00:18:41.170 align:middle line:84%
So once you've chosen
a particular model,

00:18:41.170 --> 00:18:44.810 align:middle line:84%
you can increase its complexity
and the parameters, and so on.

00:18:44.810 --> 00:18:47.090 align:middle line:84%
But as soon as it
starts to perform worse

00:18:47.090 --> 00:18:49.730 align:middle line:84%
on the validation
set, you know you've

00:18:49.730 --> 00:18:53.050 align:middle line:90%
overfitted the training set.

00:18:53.050 --> 00:18:56.890 align:middle line:84%
And then the test set
is kept to one side.

00:18:56.890 --> 00:19:02.410 align:middle line:84%
And it's not being involved
in the selection of the model

00:19:02.410 --> 00:19:03.850 align:middle line:90%
or anything like that.

00:19:03.850 --> 00:19:09.090 align:middle line:84%
And so it provides a good
final out-of-sample indication

00:19:09.090 --> 00:19:11.700 align:middle line:84%
as to how well the
chosen model works.

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:14.310 align:middle line:90%


00:19:14.310 --> 00:19:14.810 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:19:14.810 --> 00:19:17.170 align:middle line:90%
Next slide, please.

00:19:17.170 --> 00:19:19.890 align:middle line:90%
Thanks.

00:19:19.890 --> 00:19:23.990 align:middle line:84%
So this is an example
that I use in the book.

00:19:23.990 --> 00:19:27.770 align:middle line:84%
It's a pretty trivial
example in a way,

00:19:27.770 --> 00:19:30.730 align:middle line:84%
but it's for predicting
salaries for people

00:19:30.730 --> 00:19:33.210 align:middle line:84%
in a certain profession
in a certain area

00:19:33.210 --> 00:19:35.160 align:middle line:90%
as a function of age.

00:19:35.160 --> 00:19:37.010 align:middle line:90%
And we try out different models.

00:19:37.010 --> 00:19:40.120 align:middle line:90%


00:19:40.120 --> 00:19:43.000 align:middle line:84%
Didn't have a huge amount of
data for this particular one.

00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:45.200 align:middle line:84%
We try out different
models which

00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:52.920 align:middle line:84%
are just polynomials,
polynomials of the person's age.

00:19:52.920 --> 00:19:55.800 align:middle line:84%
So the first, the
extreme left model,

00:19:55.800 --> 00:19:58.360 align:middle line:90%
is a fifth order polynomial.

00:19:58.360 --> 00:20:02.160 align:middle line:90%
It fits the data pretty well.

00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:19.200 align:middle line:84%
But actually, what it's fitting
is the first 60% of data

00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:22.720 align:middle line:84%
that you use to
compare your models.

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:23.950 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:20:23.950 --> 00:20:29.080 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't fit the
validation set well.

00:20:29.080 --> 00:20:32.760 align:middle line:90%
So it's overfitting you find.

00:20:32.760 --> 00:20:34.760 align:middle line:84%
The linear model
is underfitting.

00:20:34.760 --> 00:20:38.260 align:middle line:84%
And the best model actually
for the data that I used

00:20:38.260 --> 00:20:42.820 align:middle line:90%
was a quadratic model.

00:20:42.820 --> 00:20:46.940 align:middle line:84%
So salary increases and
then slightly tails off

00:20:46.940 --> 00:20:47.750 align:middle line:90%
as you get older.

00:20:47.750 --> 00:20:51.180 align:middle line:90%


00:20:51.180 --> 00:20:54.140 align:middle line:90%
So it's an example of--

00:20:54.140 --> 00:20:57.540 align:middle line:84%
it depends how much data
you've got, of course,

00:20:57.540 --> 00:21:01.420 align:middle line:90%
for comparing your models.

00:21:01.420 --> 00:21:09.880 align:middle line:84%
But it quite often happens that
you wind up overfitting a model.

00:21:09.880 --> 00:21:21.640 align:middle line:84%
And maybe we can just go
back a slide or two to--

00:21:21.640 --> 00:21:22.140 align:middle line:90%
yeah.

00:21:22.140 --> 00:21:25.740 align:middle line:90%
So training-- sorry.

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:27.980 align:middle line:90%
So you use the training set.

00:21:27.980 --> 00:21:30.780 align:middle line:84%
But you find that
you have actually

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:32.960 align:middle line:84%
fitted the training
set really well

00:21:32.960 --> 00:21:37.920 align:middle line:84%
but the model doesn't extend
well to the validation set.

00:21:37.920 --> 00:21:41.280 align:middle line:84%
So you then say you've
overfitted the training set.

00:21:41.280 --> 00:21:45.460 align:middle line:84%
So that's an example of
what I'm talking about.

00:21:45.460 --> 00:21:48.000 align:middle line:84%
Increase model complexity
until the model

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:51.680 align:middle line:84%
starts to perform worse
on the validation set.

00:21:51.680 --> 00:21:52.460 align:middle line:90%
Thanks, Peter.

00:21:52.460 --> 00:21:54.720 align:middle line:90%
Let's move on.

00:21:54.720 --> 00:21:55.820 align:middle line:90%
Neural networks.

00:21:55.820 --> 00:21:57.420 align:middle line:84%
Well, maybe I'll
just stop there.

00:21:57.420 --> 00:22:01.400 align:middle line:84%
Are there any questions that
anybody really wants to ask

00:22:01.400 --> 00:22:04.300 align:middle line:84%
or points Anybody wants
to make before we move on?

00:22:04.300 --> 00:22:12.440 align:middle line:90%


00:22:12.440 --> 00:22:14.280 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:22:14.280 --> 00:22:21.140 align:middle line:84%
If not, OK, let's move on to the
next slide and the next slide.

00:22:21.140 --> 00:22:23.490 align:middle line:84%
So we're now going to talk
about neural networks.

00:22:23.490 --> 00:22:26.320 align:middle line:90%


00:22:26.320 --> 00:22:31.360 align:middle line:84%
And here's a picture of an
artificial neural network.

00:22:31.360 --> 00:22:34.980 align:middle line:90%
It's abbreviated ANN.

00:22:34.980 --> 00:22:40.440 align:middle line:84%
So basically, what we're trying
to do is to, in this example,

00:22:40.440 --> 00:22:43.900 align:middle line:84%
predict something from
certain variables.

00:22:43.900 --> 00:22:49.810 align:middle line:84%
So the input layer would be
the variables that we know.

00:22:49.810 --> 00:22:52.580 align:middle line:90%


00:22:52.580 --> 00:22:56.500 align:middle line:84%
And the output layer are
the one or more things

00:22:56.500 --> 00:22:57.600 align:middle line:90%
we're trying to predict.

00:22:57.600 --> 00:23:01.220 align:middle line:84%
Now I know I've got multiple
variables in the output layer

00:23:01.220 --> 00:23:01.860 align:middle line:90%
here.

00:23:01.860 --> 00:23:05.260 align:middle line:84%
Typically though, in
many applications,

00:23:05.260 --> 00:23:08.060 align:middle line:84%
there's only one thing
you're trying to predict.

00:23:08.060 --> 00:23:09.300 align:middle line:90%
So let's think in terms--

00:23:09.300 --> 00:23:12.000 align:middle line:84%
I mean, if there's multiple
things you're trying to predict,

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:14.643 align:middle line:90%
then obviously you've got to--

00:23:14.643 --> 00:23:16.060 align:middle line:84%
in terms of your
objective, you've

00:23:16.060 --> 00:23:17.820 align:middle line:84%
got to weight how
important is it

00:23:17.820 --> 00:23:19.660 align:middle line:84%
to predict this one,
how important is it

00:23:19.660 --> 00:23:21.260 align:middle line:90%
to predict that one and so on.

00:23:21.260 --> 00:23:23.500 align:middle line:84%
So let's just, for
the moment, assume

00:23:23.500 --> 00:23:27.660 align:middle line:84%
there's only one variable
in the output layer.

00:23:27.660 --> 00:23:31.400 align:middle line:84%
The models that
you're most familiar

00:23:31.400 --> 00:23:39.080 align:middle line:84%
with are models that predict
the output from the input in one

00:23:39.080 --> 00:23:41.080 align:middle line:90%
go as it were.

00:23:41.080 --> 00:23:45.690 align:middle line:84%
Linear regression would
be a classic example.

00:23:45.690 --> 00:23:48.320 align:middle line:90%


00:23:48.320 --> 00:23:54.840 align:middle line:84%
But any more complicated
model is also

00:23:54.840 --> 00:24:00.220 align:middle line:84%
likely to predict the output
directly from the input.

00:24:00.220 --> 00:24:02.960 align:middle line:84%
The key thing that
we're doing with

00:24:02.960 --> 00:24:07.240 align:middle line:84%
an artificial neural network is
we're not doing it all in one

00:24:07.240 --> 00:24:09.080 align:middle line:90%
go.

00:24:09.080 --> 00:24:15.880 align:middle line:84%
We're using the input layer,
the variables that we know,

00:24:15.880 --> 00:24:21.580 align:middle line:84%
to predict these variables in
the first hidden layer, V1,1,

00:24:21.580 --> 00:24:24.240 align:middle line:90%
V1,2, V1,3, and so on.

00:24:24.240 --> 00:24:26.800 align:middle line:84%
And then we use those variables
in the first hidden layer

00:24:26.800 --> 00:24:31.440 align:middle line:84%
to predict variables in the
second hidden layer, V2,2, V2,2,

00:24:31.440 --> 00:24:34.440 align:middle line:84%
V2,3 and so on, and then
the third hidden layer.

00:24:34.440 --> 00:24:37.420 align:middle line:90%


00:24:37.420 --> 00:24:40.420 align:middle line:84%
So the key thing is
about a neural network

00:24:40.420 --> 00:24:43.820 align:middle line:84%
is that you're going
through a number of stages

00:24:43.820 --> 00:24:47.580 align:middle line:84%
in predicting the
output from the input.

00:24:47.580 --> 00:24:49.180 align:middle line:90%
It's not all done in one go.

00:24:49.180 --> 00:24:53.260 align:middle line:84%
So we're not writing down
a nice, neat equation

00:24:53.260 --> 00:24:55.820 align:middle line:84%
for the relationship between
the output and the input

00:24:55.820 --> 00:24:59.580 align:middle line:84%
and predicting the
parameters of that equation.

00:24:59.580 --> 00:25:02.680 align:middle line:84%
We're saying there's
a number of stages.

00:25:02.680 --> 00:25:08.620 align:middle line:84%
We go through in getting
from the input to the output.

00:25:08.620 --> 00:25:16.810 align:middle line:84%
And that makes the model, of
course, a lot more flexible.

00:25:16.810 --> 00:25:19.420 align:middle line:90%


00:25:19.420 --> 00:25:19.970 align:middle line:90%
Next slide.

00:25:19.970 --> 00:25:22.500 align:middle line:90%


00:25:22.500 --> 00:25:31.450 align:middle line:84%
So what's the relationship
between the values in one layer

00:25:31.450 --> 00:25:33.350 align:middle line:84%
and the values in
a previous layer?

00:25:33.350 --> 00:25:35.890 align:middle line:90%


00:25:35.890 --> 00:25:41.770 align:middle line:84%
Well, what we're doing is
we're relating the values

00:25:41.770 --> 00:25:46.730 align:middle line:84%
at one neuron to a linear
combination of values

00:25:46.730 --> 00:25:48.570 align:middle line:90%
in the previous layer.

00:25:48.570 --> 00:25:53.530 align:middle line:84%
And we're using these things
called activation functions

00:25:53.530 --> 00:25:54.030 align:middle line:90%
to do it.

00:25:54.030 --> 00:25:57.850 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we go back a slide
now, Peter, if we could.

00:25:57.850 --> 00:26:05.410 align:middle line:84%
And so if we look here, V1,1
is related to the variables

00:26:05.410 --> 00:26:08.850 align:middle line:84%
in the input layer, the
variables we know, V0,1, V0,2,

00:26:08.850 --> 00:26:10.330 align:middle line:90%
V0,3.

00:26:10.330 --> 00:26:14.090 align:middle line:84%
And what we do-- what we
do is we apply weights

00:26:14.090 --> 00:26:16.130 align:middle line:90%
to those variables.

00:26:16.130 --> 00:26:22.210 align:middle line:84%
So we have a weight times
V0,1 plus a weight times V02,

00:26:22.210 --> 00:26:25.270 align:middle line:84%
plus a weight times
V0,3 and so on.

00:26:25.270 --> 00:26:28.310 align:middle line:90%


00:26:28.310 --> 00:26:33.796 align:middle line:84%
And then once we got this
weighted average of the V01,

00:26:33.796 --> 00:26:39.850 align:middle line:84%
V0,2, V0,3, we apply a
function to it to get V1,1.

00:26:39.850 --> 00:26:42.550 align:middle line:84%
And then we do the
same thing for V1,2.

00:26:42.550 --> 00:26:48.330 align:middle line:84%
We take a weighted
average of V0,1, V0,2,

00:26:48.330 --> 00:26:52.150 align:middle line:84%
V0,3 and so on, different
weighted average,

00:26:52.150 --> 00:26:58.950 align:middle line:84%
and apply a function to
it to get V1,2 and so on.

00:26:58.950 --> 00:27:05.103 align:middle line:84%
And then to go from the V1,1's
V1,2's, V1,3's to V2,1, V2,2,

00:27:05.103 --> 00:27:07.750 align:middle line:90%
V2,3, we do the same thing.

00:27:07.750 --> 00:27:16.474 align:middle line:84%
So V2,1, for example, we take a
weighted average of V1,1, V1,2,

00:27:16.474 --> 00:27:25.010 align:middle line:84%
V1,3, V1,4 and so on, and
apply a function to it,

00:27:25.010 --> 00:27:28.410 align:middle line:84%
this activation
function to get V2,1.

00:27:28.410 --> 00:27:30.730 align:middle line:84%
And then so what
are the things--

00:27:30.730 --> 00:27:32.508 align:middle line:84%
what are the parameters
of this model?

00:27:32.508 --> 00:27:33.800 align:middle line:90%
The parameters are the weights.

00:27:33.800 --> 00:27:36.970 align:middle line:90%


00:27:36.970 --> 00:27:43.850 align:middle line:84%
What weights do we apply to
the V's in going from one layer

00:27:43.850 --> 00:27:45.730 align:middle line:90%
to the next layer?

00:27:45.730 --> 00:27:47.050 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:27:47.050 --> 00:27:51.410 align:middle line:84%
And there's a lot of weights
in this example that you got

00:27:51.410 --> 00:27:55.410 align:middle line:84%
in front of you there because
when we're determining V1,1,

00:27:55.410 --> 00:28:00.330 align:middle line:84%
we've got a weight associated
with V0,1, V0,2, V0,3,

00:28:00.330 --> 00:28:01.150 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

00:28:01.150 --> 00:28:03.730 align:middle line:90%


00:28:03.730 --> 00:28:08.130 align:middle line:84%
But even if there are only three
inputs, that's three weights.

00:28:08.130 --> 00:28:11.250 align:middle line:84%
And then V1,2 is
another three weights.

00:28:11.250 --> 00:28:13.970 align:middle line:84%
V1,3 is another three
weights and so on.

00:28:13.970 --> 00:28:16.210 align:middle line:84%
So you can wind up
with a lot of weights.

00:28:16.210 --> 00:28:18.810 align:middle line:84%
But those are the
variables that--

00:28:18.810 --> 00:28:24.190 align:middle line:84%
those weights are the variables
you're trying to determine.

00:28:24.190 --> 00:28:26.590 align:middle line:84%
And then the
activation function is

00:28:26.590 --> 00:28:31.370 align:middle line:84%
something you apply after
you've applied the weights.

00:28:31.370 --> 00:28:31.870 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:28:31.870 --> 00:28:34.990 align:middle line:84%
So can we move on to
the next slide now?

00:28:34.990 --> 00:28:41.390 align:middle line:84%
So you see a number of
different activation functions

00:28:41.390 --> 00:28:44.030 align:middle line:90%
that you could use.

00:28:44.030 --> 00:28:48.350 align:middle line:84%
fy equals y means that
you've just applied weights

00:28:48.350 --> 00:28:50.170 align:middle line:84%
to the variables in
the previous layer.

00:28:50.170 --> 00:28:52.970 align:middle line:84%
And then you've just haven't
done anything else to them.

00:28:52.970 --> 00:28:54.990 align:middle line:84%
You've just said that
that's going to give you

00:28:54.990 --> 00:28:57.430 align:middle line:90%
a value in the new layer.

00:28:57.430 --> 00:29:04.530 align:middle line:84%
Now if, throughout the
whole neural network,

00:29:04.530 --> 00:29:07.770 align:middle line:84%
you just use the identity,
if you think about it,

00:29:07.770 --> 00:29:11.030 align:middle line:90%
you just have a linear model.

00:29:11.030 --> 00:29:12.630 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:29:12.630 --> 00:29:17.790 align:middle line:84%
So if the variables
in the first layer

00:29:17.790 --> 00:29:20.710 align:middle line:84%
were a linear function
of the input layer,

00:29:20.710 --> 00:29:23.270 align:middle line:84%
the variables in
the second layer

00:29:23.270 --> 00:29:26.730 align:middle line:84%
were linear functions of the
variables in the first layer

00:29:26.730 --> 00:29:29.390 align:middle line:84%
and so on, and you went
all the way through,

00:29:29.390 --> 00:29:31.190 align:middle line:84%
then it would just
be a linear model.

00:29:31.190 --> 00:29:32.810 align:middle line:84%
And that wouldn't
be very interesting

00:29:32.810 --> 00:29:34.560 align:middle line:84%
because if we want to
have a linear model,

00:29:34.560 --> 00:29:36.250 align:middle line:84%
we don't have to go
through all this.

00:29:36.250 --> 00:29:41.690 align:middle line:84%
However, as we'll see,
you often use the identity

00:29:41.690 --> 00:29:44.530 align:middle line:90%
just for the final layer.

00:29:44.530 --> 00:29:50.450 align:middle line:84%
You see sigmoid is a
popular activation function.

00:29:50.450 --> 00:30:01.450 align:middle line:84%
So once you've determined
the weighted average

00:30:01.450 --> 00:30:04.170 align:middle line:84%
of the variables in
the previous layer,

00:30:04.170 --> 00:30:08.930 align:middle line:84%
you take 1 minus 1
plus e to the minus

00:30:08.930 --> 00:30:14.890 align:middle line:84%
that to get the value of
the new node and so on.

00:30:14.890 --> 00:30:17.670 align:middle line:84%
Hyperbolic tangent,
ReLU and so on.

00:30:17.670 --> 00:30:20.410 align:middle line:84%
Lots of different
activation functions.

00:30:20.410 --> 00:30:23.800 align:middle line:90%
So you get the idea.

00:30:23.800 --> 00:30:29.320 align:middle line:84%
You're really saying that the
relationship between the outputs

00:30:29.320 --> 00:30:34.920 align:middle line:84%
and the input is a
kind of a convolution

00:30:34.920 --> 00:30:41.000 align:middle line:84%
of a lot of these
activation functions applied

00:30:41.000 --> 00:30:43.940 align:middle line:90%
to weighted averages.

00:30:43.940 --> 00:30:44.440 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:30:44.440 --> 00:30:44.940 align:middle line:90%
Next.

00:30:44.940 --> 00:30:49.040 align:middle line:90%


00:30:49.040 --> 00:30:58.640 align:middle line:84%
So how do we actually
solve the model?

00:30:58.640 --> 00:31:08.520 align:middle line:84%
Well, basically, you've
got, typically, in models,

00:31:08.520 --> 00:31:10.480 align:middle line:84%
you've got lots of
parameter values, all

00:31:10.480 --> 00:31:14.760 align:middle line:84%
the different weights
that you're applying.

00:31:14.760 --> 00:31:21.540 align:middle line:84%
And you say, well, you start
off by assuming some values

00:31:21.540 --> 00:31:24.000 align:middle line:84%
for the weights, probably
not very good values,

00:31:24.000 --> 00:31:25.720 align:middle line:90%
but you assume some values.

00:31:25.720 --> 00:31:27.400 align:middle line:84%
And then you
calculate gradients.

00:31:27.400 --> 00:31:34.820 align:middle line:84%
You say, well, perhaps we're
trying to minimize some value

00:31:34.820 --> 00:31:37.060 align:middle line:90%
or minimize a function.

00:31:37.060 --> 00:31:40.540 align:middle line:84%
So we're trying to determine
the input values that

00:31:40.540 --> 00:31:42.820 align:middle line:90%
will minimize some function.

00:31:42.820 --> 00:31:46.180 align:middle line:84%
So we calculate gradients to
determine the direction in which

00:31:46.180 --> 00:31:50.940 align:middle line:84%
the parameter values
should be changed

00:31:50.940 --> 00:31:55.380 align:middle line:84%
to improve things to actually
make the function smaller.

00:31:55.380 --> 00:31:59.920 align:middle line:84%
And then you take a step and
then do the whole thing again,

00:31:59.920 --> 00:32:07.580 align:middle line:84%
calculate the gradients, and
move the parameter values

00:32:07.580 --> 00:32:12.140 align:middle line:84%
again in a certain
direction and so on.

00:32:12.140 --> 00:32:16.480 align:middle line:90%
So it's a case of--

00:32:16.480 --> 00:32:19.172 align:middle line:90%


00:32:19.172 --> 00:32:20.380 align:middle line:90%
somebody's trying to call me.

00:32:20.380 --> 00:32:23.120 align:middle line:90%


00:32:23.120 --> 00:32:29.080 align:middle line:84%
So basically, what you
need is you're trying

00:32:29.080 --> 00:32:30.780 align:middle line:90%
to minimize some function.

00:32:30.780 --> 00:32:33.200 align:middle line:84%
What you need is the
partial derivative

00:32:33.200 --> 00:32:37.320 align:middle line:84%
of that function with respect
to all these parameters, all

00:32:37.320 --> 00:32:41.160 align:middle line:84%
these weights that
define your model.

00:32:41.160 --> 00:32:43.920 align:middle line:84%
Now that seems a
pretty tricky task.

00:32:43.920 --> 00:32:50.080 align:middle line:90%
But actually, there was--

00:32:50.080 --> 00:32:53.800 align:middle line:84%
I mean, clever maybe, you
could say almost obvious way

00:32:53.800 --> 00:32:58.000 align:middle line:84%
of doing this is to
say, what we're really

00:32:58.000 --> 00:33:04.000 align:middle line:84%
talking about with this model
is the final thing that we're

00:33:04.000 --> 00:33:08.080 align:middle line:84%
interested in being a function
of a function of a function.

00:33:08.080 --> 00:33:16.320 align:middle line:84%
In other words, if we're a
function of the final weights

00:33:16.320 --> 00:33:19.020 align:middle line:84%
and those final
weights are a function

00:33:19.020 --> 00:33:23.060 align:middle line:84%
of the weights that
come immediately

00:33:23.060 --> 00:33:26.660 align:middle line:84%
before the final weights and
then those final weights.

00:33:26.660 --> 00:33:33.580 align:middle line:84%
So each weight-- and
of course, in calculus,

00:33:33.580 --> 00:33:37.740 align:middle line:84%
we know how to calculate
the gradient of a function

00:33:37.740 --> 00:33:39.300 align:middle line:90%
of a function of a function.

00:33:39.300 --> 00:33:45.180 align:middle line:84%
So there are some
clever shortcuts

00:33:45.180 --> 00:33:48.740 align:middle line:84%
for calculating these
gradients that you

00:33:48.740 --> 00:33:54.820 align:middle line:84%
need to move the parameters
in the right direction.

00:33:54.820 --> 00:33:57.500 align:middle line:84%
They're not starting
from scratch

00:33:57.500 --> 00:33:59.490 align:middle line:84%
in terms of calculating
each gradient.

00:33:59.490 --> 00:34:03.940 align:middle line:90%


00:34:03.940 --> 00:34:05.520 align:middle line:90%
Next slide, please.

00:34:05.520 --> 00:34:09.219 align:middle line:90%


00:34:09.219 --> 00:34:12.980 align:middle line:84%
So this is a very simple example
but it illustrates the nature

00:34:12.980 --> 00:34:14.000 align:middle line:90%
of what you're doing.

00:34:14.000 --> 00:34:17.280 align:middle line:84%
I mean let's suppose you wanted
to calculate the value of x that

00:34:17.280 --> 00:34:24.040 align:middle line:84%
minimizes this function, y
equals 6x squared minus 8x plus

00:34:24.040 --> 00:34:24.540 align:middle line:90%
20.

00:34:24.540 --> 00:34:28.179 align:middle line:84%
Of course, we could all do this
with calculus very quickly.

00:34:28.179 --> 00:34:30.060 align:middle line:90%
And x equals 4 is the answer.

00:34:30.060 --> 00:34:34.639 align:middle line:84%
But suppose we were
doing it in the way

00:34:34.639 --> 00:34:38.120 align:middle line:90%
that I've just described.

00:34:38.120 --> 00:34:41.380 align:middle line:84%
Then we'd start with
some estimate of x.

00:34:41.380 --> 00:34:45.199 align:middle line:84%
It might be x equals 1,
not a very good estimate.

00:34:45.199 --> 00:34:53.220 align:middle line:84%
And then we'd look
at the gradient

00:34:53.220 --> 00:34:56.719 align:middle line:90%
of this, when x equals 1.

00:34:56.719 --> 00:34:57.980 align:middle line:90%
And what is it?

00:34:57.980 --> 00:35:00.940 align:middle line:90%
It's 2x minus 8.

00:35:00.940 --> 00:35:04.170 align:middle line:90%
So it's the gradient is minus 6.

00:35:04.170 --> 00:35:09.680 align:middle line:90%


00:35:09.680 --> 00:35:13.200 align:middle line:84%
We move the value
that we're looking

00:35:13.200 --> 00:35:20.300 align:middle line:84%
at in the direction,
which minimizes

00:35:20.300 --> 00:35:24.260 align:middle line:84%
y, which the
gradient was minus 6

00:35:24.260 --> 00:35:26.420 align:middle line:84%
so we take the negative
of the gradient

00:35:26.420 --> 00:35:32.140 align:middle line:84%
because we're trying to
minimize something and move it

00:35:32.140 --> 00:35:36.220 align:middle line:84%
in the direction
indicated by the gradient

00:35:36.220 --> 00:35:37.900 align:middle line:90%
by a certain amount.

00:35:37.900 --> 00:35:40.460 align:middle line:84%
And we see that we're then
at a little bit more than 2.

00:35:40.460 --> 00:35:42.900 align:middle line:84%
Then we calculate
the gradient again,

00:35:42.900 --> 00:35:45.460 align:middle line:84%
move it again, calculate
gradient again.

00:35:45.460 --> 00:35:48.060 align:middle line:84%
So you can see how
this method would

00:35:48.060 --> 00:35:56.580 align:middle line:84%
work in this very simple example
where there's only one thing

00:35:56.580 --> 00:36:00.300 align:middle line:90%
that we're having to estimate.

00:36:00.300 --> 00:36:00.880 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:36:00.880 --> 00:36:06.940 align:middle line:84%
And obviously, when there's
many things we want to estimate,

00:36:06.940 --> 00:36:09.540 align:middle line:84%
it's the same basic
principle except we're

00:36:09.540 --> 00:36:11.780 align:middle line:90%
working in many dimensions.

00:36:11.780 --> 00:36:13.370 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:36:13.370 --> 00:36:14.550 align:middle line:90%
Next slide, please.

00:36:14.550 --> 00:36:17.490 align:middle line:90%


00:36:17.490 --> 00:36:19.930 align:middle line:84%
So we continue
training the model

00:36:19.930 --> 00:36:23.010 align:middle line:84%
until the results of the
validation set diverge

00:36:23.010 --> 00:36:24.550 align:middle line:90%
from those of the training set.

00:36:24.550 --> 00:36:26.770 align:middle line:90%
We don't want to overtrain.

00:36:26.770 --> 00:36:28.950 align:middle line:84%
Choosing the learning
rate is important.

00:36:28.950 --> 00:36:31.670 align:middle line:84%
The learning rate
is how far we move.

00:36:31.670 --> 00:36:37.610 align:middle line:84%
Once we've estimated
the gradient,

00:36:37.610 --> 00:36:40.690 align:middle line:84%
we have to decide
how far we want

00:36:40.690 --> 00:36:44.250 align:middle line:84%
to move in the direction
indicated by the gradient.

00:36:44.250 --> 00:36:55.090 align:middle line:84%
And well, there are some clever
ways of determining the learning

00:36:55.090 --> 00:36:57.130 align:middle line:90%
rate as it's called.

00:36:57.130 --> 00:36:59.890 align:middle line:84%
But clearly, if we could just
go back to the previous slide

00:36:59.890 --> 00:37:04.150 align:middle line:84%
a moment, I think
you can see here,

00:37:04.150 --> 00:37:09.610 align:middle line:84%
if you choose a really
small learning rate,

00:37:09.610 --> 00:37:12.930 align:middle line:84%
then the gradient at
1 would be the same

00:37:12.930 --> 00:37:15.970 align:middle line:84%
but you'd move only
a very short amount,

00:37:15.970 --> 00:37:18.510 align:middle line:84%
and then you calculate the
gradient again, and move

00:37:18.510 --> 00:37:20.330 align:middle line:90%
a very short amount, and so on.

00:37:20.330 --> 00:37:22.750 align:middle line:84%
You'd never actually
reach the bottom of the--

00:37:22.750 --> 00:37:25.810 align:middle line:84%
you'd never in a reasonable time
reach the bottom of the curve

00:37:25.810 --> 00:37:28.830 align:middle line:84%
if you made the learning rate
too small because the learning

00:37:28.830 --> 00:37:32.510 align:middle line:84%
rate is determining how
far you move once you've

00:37:32.510 --> 00:37:33.885 align:middle line:90%
determined the gradient.

00:37:33.885 --> 00:37:36.010 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand, if the
learning rate is too big,

00:37:36.010 --> 00:37:39.390 align:middle line:84%
you could oscillate and
move from above the minimum

00:37:39.390 --> 00:37:42.510 align:middle line:84%
to below the minimum to
above the minimum to below

00:37:42.510 --> 00:37:44.910 align:middle line:90%
the minimum, and so on.

00:37:44.910 --> 00:37:49.590 align:middle line:84%
So determining the
learning rate is important.

00:37:49.590 --> 00:37:51.530 align:middle line:84%
You can do it by
trial and error.

00:37:51.530 --> 00:37:58.870 align:middle line:84%
As I say, there's some clever
ways of doing it as well.

00:37:58.870 --> 00:37:59.870 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:37:59.870 --> 00:38:02.710 align:middle line:90%
Next slide, please.

00:38:02.710 --> 00:38:08.080 align:middle line:84%
It's also turns out to be
best to scale the variables

00:38:08.080 --> 00:38:09.330 align:middle line:90%
when you're doing all of this.

00:38:09.330 --> 00:38:11.250 align:middle line:84%
And backpropagation
is used to calculate

00:38:11.250 --> 00:38:12.650 align:middle line:90%
the partial derivatives.

00:38:12.650 --> 00:38:16.650 align:middle line:84%
That's what I mentioned before,
which is really just saying

00:38:16.650 --> 00:38:18.323 align:middle line:90%
the partial derivatives.

00:38:18.323 --> 00:38:20.990 align:middle line:84%
You're talking about taking the
partial derivative of something,

00:38:20.990 --> 00:38:22.890 align:middle line:84%
which is a function
of a function.

00:38:22.890 --> 00:38:26.710 align:middle line:84%
We've known how to do that in
calculus for quite a long time.

00:38:26.710 --> 00:38:27.210 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:38:27.210 --> 00:38:30.610 align:middle line:90%
Next slide, please.

00:38:30.610 --> 00:38:36.450 align:middle line:84%
So here's one application
that we've used it for.

00:38:36.450 --> 00:38:39.610 align:middle line:84%
May seem a little artificial,
but actually it's not,

00:38:39.610 --> 00:38:40.870 align:middle line:90%
as I'll explain in a minute.

00:38:40.870 --> 00:38:47.730 align:middle line:84%
But basically, I generated
10,000 call option prices using

00:38:47.730 --> 00:38:52.410 align:middle line:84%
the Black-Scholes-Merton model,
and added a normally distributed

00:38:52.410 --> 00:38:53.410 align:middle line:90%
error.

00:38:53.410 --> 00:38:55.390 align:middle line:90%
So I generated a lot of data.

00:38:55.390 --> 00:39:03.090 align:middle line:84%
I said that the asset prices,
the strike price K, the risk

00:39:03.090 --> 00:39:11.630 align:middle line:84%
free rate, the volatility
sigma and the time to maturity.

00:39:11.630 --> 00:39:13.230 align:middle line:84%
You can see in the
second bullet point

00:39:13.230 --> 00:39:19.230 align:middle line:84%
there what I chose
as the ranges there.

00:39:19.230 --> 00:39:22.790 align:middle line:84%
So we sampled
parameters randomly

00:39:22.790 --> 00:39:28.510 align:middle line:84%
and then added a normally
distributed error term

00:39:28.510 --> 00:39:30.470 align:middle line:84%
to the
Black-Scholes-Merton model

00:39:30.470 --> 00:39:36.510 align:middle line:84%
and then said, let's see how
well the model can actually

00:39:36.510 --> 00:39:43.710 align:middle line:84%
determine what the
function is and actually

00:39:43.710 --> 00:39:47.490 align:middle line:84%
used three hidden layers,
20 neurons per layer

00:39:47.490 --> 00:39:55.910 align:middle line:84%
and sigmoid activation function
except the final layer.

00:39:55.910 --> 00:39:57.090 align:middle line:90%
And this is a point.

00:39:57.090 --> 00:40:00.250 align:middle line:84%
If I use the sigmoid
activation function everywhere,

00:40:00.250 --> 00:40:04.950 align:middle line:84%
it wouldn't have worked
because sigmoid activation

00:40:04.950 --> 00:40:08.650 align:middle line:84%
function gives you a
value between 0 and 1.

00:40:08.650 --> 00:40:11.330 align:middle line:84%
And of course, the
Black-Scholes-Merton price

00:40:11.330 --> 00:40:13.370 align:middle line:90%
is not between 0 and 1.

00:40:13.370 --> 00:40:19.250 align:middle line:90%
So I used the.

00:40:19.250 --> 00:40:23.710 align:middle line:84%
identity activation function
for the final layer.

00:40:23.710 --> 00:40:27.010 align:middle line:84%
And that's not uncommon to
use the identity activation

00:40:27.010 --> 00:40:30.930 align:middle line:84%
function for the final layer
because the activation functions

00:40:30.930 --> 00:40:34.170 align:middle line:84%
you're using for earlier
layers may be constraining

00:40:34.170 --> 00:40:37.410 align:middle line:90%
the answer you get.

00:40:37.410 --> 00:40:41.450 align:middle line:84%
And divided the observation
between the training set,

00:40:41.450 --> 00:40:45.370 align:middle line:84%
and validation set, and the
test set in the way that we were

00:40:45.370 --> 00:40:48.710 align:middle line:84%
discussing earlier, 6,000
for the training set,

00:40:48.710 --> 00:40:55.850 align:middle line:84%
2,000 for the validation
set, 2,000 for the test set.

00:40:55.850 --> 00:40:59.930 align:middle line:84%
We have the next
slide, please, Peter.

00:40:59.930 --> 00:41:05.650 align:middle line:90%
So you can see what happens.

00:41:05.650 --> 00:41:07.900 align:middle line:84%
The epochs of the
training set is increased,

00:41:07.900 --> 00:41:10.880 align:middle line:84%
which just basically
means we continue

00:41:10.880 --> 00:41:16.960 align:middle line:84%
to try and match the training
set data as closely as possible.

00:41:16.960 --> 00:41:23.200 align:middle line:84%
It improves things for
the validation set up

00:41:23.200 --> 00:41:27.300 align:middle line:84%
to a certain point and
then starts to get worse.

00:41:27.300 --> 00:41:31.600 align:middle line:90%
See what's going on better.

00:41:31.600 --> 00:41:34.360 align:middle line:90%
Next slide.

00:41:34.360 --> 00:41:38.440 align:middle line:90%
We can take a moving average.

00:41:38.440 --> 00:41:41.360 align:middle line:84%
And this is-- so you can
see what's going on here.

00:41:41.360 --> 00:41:44.800 align:middle line:84%
We keep trying to
improve the model.

00:41:44.800 --> 00:41:49.100 align:middle line:90%
But we stop after 2,575 epochs.

00:41:49.100 --> 00:41:51.480 align:middle line:84%
Just think about the
epoch as a trial.

00:41:51.480 --> 00:42:02.480 align:middle line:84%
So you can see that things
improve for the validation set

00:42:02.480 --> 00:42:03.580 align:middle line:90%
up to a certain point.

00:42:03.580 --> 00:42:08.500 align:middle line:84%
And then they seem to get
worse so it's best to stop.

00:42:08.500 --> 00:42:11.300 align:middle line:84%
So the model that we actually
wound up with was the model

00:42:11.300 --> 00:42:20.140 align:middle line:90%
after 2,575 epochs.

00:42:20.140 --> 00:42:22.260 align:middle line:90%
Next slide, please.

00:42:22.260 --> 00:42:26.900 align:middle line:84%
So we only had
10,000 observations.

00:42:26.900 --> 00:42:29.860 align:middle line:84%
But we found that the
neural network actually

00:42:29.860 --> 00:42:32.140 align:middle line:84%
imitated the
Black-Scholes-Merton model

00:42:32.140 --> 00:42:33.740 align:middle line:90%
very well.

00:42:33.740 --> 00:42:37.020 align:middle line:84%
In other words, the
random noise that we added

00:42:37.020 --> 00:42:41.100 align:middle line:84%
to the Black-Scholes-Merton
prices didn't-- it managed

00:42:41.100 --> 00:42:46.180 align:middle line:84%
to overcome that random
noise pretty well.

00:42:46.180 --> 00:42:47.940 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:42:47.940 --> 00:42:51.515 align:middle line:84%
There's more details
about that in my book.

00:42:51.515 --> 00:42:55.020 align:middle line:90%
So the next slide.

00:42:55.020 --> 00:42:58.040 align:middle line:84%
Now you may say, well, that's
a pretty artificial example.

00:42:58.040 --> 00:43:02.300 align:middle line:90%
We don't need a model to--

00:43:02.300 --> 00:43:04.600 align:middle line:84%
we don't need to
use neural networks

00:43:04.600 --> 00:43:08.960 align:middle line:84%
to determine the Black-Scholes
model because we have a formula.

00:43:08.960 --> 00:43:13.560 align:middle line:84%
But it turns out that,
actually, derivatives companies

00:43:13.560 --> 00:43:19.360 align:middle line:84%
are using neural networks
to value exotic derivatives.

00:43:19.360 --> 00:43:21.600 align:middle line:84%
There are some derivatives
which can only be valued

00:43:21.600 --> 00:43:24.320 align:middle line:90%
using Monte Carlo simulation.

00:43:24.320 --> 00:43:26.260 align:middle line:84%
And the Monte Carlo
simulation is slow.

00:43:26.260 --> 00:43:30.100 align:middle line:84%
It can take three or four
minutes to come up with a price.

00:43:30.100 --> 00:43:32.720 align:middle line:90%


00:43:32.720 --> 00:43:35.060 align:middle line:84%
If you've got a client
on the end of the line,

00:43:35.060 --> 00:43:37.480 align:middle line:90%
that may not be very good.

00:43:37.480 --> 00:43:42.160 align:middle line:84%
So basically,
instead of doing this

00:43:42.160 --> 00:43:44.840 align:middle line:84%
for the
Black-Scholes-Merton model,

00:43:44.840 --> 00:43:49.040 align:middle line:84%
you could do it for some
exotic derivatives model.

00:43:49.040 --> 00:43:52.760 align:middle line:84%
I mean, you run your Monte Carlo
simulation many, many times

00:43:52.760 --> 00:44:01.640 align:middle line:84%
to create data, which relates
the price to the inputs.

00:44:01.640 --> 00:44:03.300 align:middle line:84%
And then you create
a neural network

00:44:03.300 --> 00:44:07.300 align:middle line:84%
to replicate the prices
as closely as possible.

00:44:07.300 --> 00:44:11.380 align:middle line:84%
And then after you've done that,
you have a really fast way--

00:44:11.380 --> 00:44:13.540 align:middle line:90%
I mean, obviously you don't--

00:44:13.540 --> 00:44:17.140 align:middle line:84%
if you're doing this in the
way that I'm describing,

00:44:17.140 --> 00:44:20.780 align:middle line:84%
you wouldn't actually
add an error term to it.

00:44:20.780 --> 00:44:22.380 align:middle line:84%
You just want to
create the prices

00:44:22.380 --> 00:44:24.260 align:middle line:90%
as accurately as possible.

00:44:24.260 --> 00:44:30.660 align:middle line:84%
And once you've got a neural
network that will actually

00:44:30.660 --> 00:44:33.820 align:middle line:84%
create these exotic
option prices,

00:44:33.820 --> 00:44:37.500 align:middle line:84%
you can then very, very
quickly calculate any new price

00:44:37.500 --> 00:44:40.540 align:middle line:84%
you want to because
all you're doing

00:44:40.540 --> 00:44:45.460 align:middle line:84%
to work to calculate a new
price is working forward

00:44:45.460 --> 00:44:48.740 align:middle line:90%
through the neural network.

00:44:48.740 --> 00:44:53.900 align:middle line:84%
So instead of being
three or four minutes,

00:44:53.900 --> 00:45:01.640 align:middle line:84%
it's almost instantaneous
because, as I say,

00:45:01.640 --> 00:45:04.720 align:middle line:84%
you work forward through
the neural network

00:45:04.720 --> 00:45:06.180 align:middle line:90%
to obtain the price.

00:45:06.180 --> 00:45:09.460 align:middle line:84%
And this can also be useful
for scenario analysis

00:45:09.460 --> 00:45:13.360 align:middle line:84%
because scenario analysis
can be very slow if you're

00:45:13.360 --> 00:45:17.680 align:middle line:84%
having to calculate this exotic
option over and over again using

00:45:17.680 --> 00:45:19.720 align:middle line:90%
Monte Carlo simulation.

00:45:19.720 --> 00:45:26.040 align:middle line:84%
But if you've constructed
a neural network

00:45:26.040 --> 00:45:32.160 align:middle line:84%
to produce a price,
then it's much faster

00:45:32.160 --> 00:45:35.910 align:middle line:84%
and scenario analysis
is perfectly feasible.

00:45:35.910 --> 00:45:38.520 align:middle line:90%


00:45:38.520 --> 00:45:42.020 align:middle line:84%
Can we have the
next slide, please?

00:45:42.020 --> 00:45:45.520 align:middle line:90%


00:45:45.520 --> 00:45:48.040 align:middle line:84%
Another thing we've looked
at as part of our research

00:45:48.040 --> 00:45:53.440 align:middle line:84%
is we've looked at
the relationship

00:45:53.440 --> 00:45:57.240 align:middle line:84%
between volatilities
and how volatilities

00:45:57.240 --> 00:46:00.990 align:middle line:84%
change as the return
on the asset changes.

00:46:00.990 --> 00:46:04.380 align:middle line:90%


00:46:04.380 --> 00:46:11.160 align:middle line:84%
The standard way of doing
hedging, of course, is to say,

00:46:11.160 --> 00:46:14.560 align:middle line:84%
well, I mean, let's talk
about delta hedging,

00:46:14.560 --> 00:46:17.540 align:middle line:84%
but it applies to
other hedging as well.

00:46:17.540 --> 00:46:22.300 align:middle line:84%
With delta hedging,
you say, well,

00:46:22.300 --> 00:46:27.380 align:middle line:84%
how does the price
of the option change

00:46:27.380 --> 00:46:29.933 align:middle line:84%
when the price of the
underlying asset changes?

00:46:29.933 --> 00:46:32.100 align:middle line:84%
And we'll assume that the
implied volatility remains

00:46:32.100 --> 00:46:34.060 align:middle line:90%
the same.

00:46:34.060 --> 00:46:35.900 align:middle line:90%
That's the standard assumption.

00:46:35.900 --> 00:46:37.780 align:middle line:84%
Implied volatility
remains the same.

00:46:37.780 --> 00:46:43.900 align:middle line:84%
And you just calculate
delta as the rate

00:46:43.900 --> 00:46:49.150 align:middle line:84%
of change of the option price
with respect to the asset price.

00:46:49.150 --> 00:46:52.340 align:middle line:90%


00:46:52.340 --> 00:46:57.500 align:middle line:84%
But perhaps there's a
tendency for the volatility

00:46:57.500 --> 00:47:03.750 align:middle line:84%
to change as the
asset price changes.

00:47:03.750 --> 00:47:06.910 align:middle line:84%
In other words, if there's a
positive return on the asset

00:47:06.910 --> 00:47:14.850 align:middle line:84%
so the asset price goes
up, there's less overall--

00:47:14.850 --> 00:47:16.740 align:middle line:84%
the company is less
highly levered.

00:47:16.740 --> 00:47:19.290 align:middle line:90%


00:47:19.290 --> 00:47:21.350 align:middle line:84%
At least in the
market value sense,

00:47:21.350 --> 00:47:23.170 align:middle line:84%
the company is
less highly levered

00:47:23.170 --> 00:47:26.810 align:middle line:84%
and so maybe the implied
volatility goes down

00:47:26.810 --> 00:47:32.570 align:middle line:84%
whereas, if the return
on the asset goes down,

00:47:32.570 --> 00:47:34.650 align:middle line:84%
the company becomes
more highly levered

00:47:34.650 --> 00:47:37.970 align:middle line:84%
and therefore this tendency
for the volatility to goes up.

00:47:37.970 --> 00:47:41.210 align:middle line:84%
So maybe we should
take this into account

00:47:41.210 --> 00:47:45.050 align:middle line:90%
when calculating delta.

00:47:45.050 --> 00:47:49.290 align:middle line:84%
So can we have the next
slide, please, Peter?

00:47:49.290 --> 00:48:01.990 align:middle line:84%
So we calculated a neural
network to investigate this

00:48:01.990 --> 00:48:03.910 align:middle line:84%
and to see what's
the relationship

00:48:03.910 --> 00:48:07.590 align:middle line:84%
between the daily asset
price return and the change

00:48:07.590 --> 00:48:10.990 align:middle line:90%
in the implied volatility.

00:48:10.990 --> 00:48:17.550 align:middle line:84%
And so input layer had
moneyness, time to maturity,

00:48:17.550 --> 00:48:19.490 align:middle line:90%
daily asset price return.

00:48:19.490 --> 00:48:23.190 align:middle line:84%
And the output layer had
change in implied volatility.

00:48:23.190 --> 00:48:26.790 align:middle line:90%
Next slide, please.

00:48:26.790 --> 00:48:33.470 align:middle line:90%
These were all the details.

00:48:33.470 --> 00:48:35.490 align:middle line:84%
In the interest of
time, let's move on.

00:48:35.490 --> 00:48:38.350 align:middle line:90%
Next slide, please.

00:48:38.350 --> 00:48:50.150 align:middle line:84%
And we can see that we actually,
in this particular case,

00:48:50.150 --> 00:48:57.170 align:middle line:84%
found that validation sets
started to get worse after 5,826

00:48:57.170 --> 00:48:57.830 align:middle line:90%
epochs.

00:48:57.830 --> 00:49:02.770 align:middle line:84%
You kind of see that a little
bit in this diagram so we

00:49:02.770 --> 00:49:07.730 align:middle line:84%
stopped the training
after 5,826 epochs.

00:49:07.730 --> 00:49:08.360 align:middle line:90%
Next slide.

00:49:08.360 --> 00:49:15.522 align:middle line:90%


00:49:15.522 --> 00:49:17.730 align:middle line:84%
At first, we were pretty
disappointed with the result

00:49:17.730 --> 00:49:25.270 align:middle line:84%
of this research because the
test set, which is final result,

00:49:25.270 --> 00:49:28.050 align:middle line:84%
it actually only gave
an 11% improvement

00:49:28.050 --> 00:49:32.330 align:middle line:84%
over a simpler analytic model
that my colleague Alan White

00:49:32.330 --> 00:49:35.330 align:middle line:90%
and I had worked on in 2017.

00:49:35.330 --> 00:49:42.170 align:middle line:84%
So it didn't give a
hugely better relationship

00:49:42.170 --> 00:49:45.970 align:middle line:84%
between the return on the
asset and the volatility.

00:49:45.970 --> 00:49:49.110 align:middle line:84%
However, we played
around with it a bit.

00:49:49.110 --> 00:49:54.330 align:middle line:84%
And when the VIX index
was used as a feature,

00:49:54.330 --> 00:49:58.470 align:middle line:84%
there's a further improvement
of 60% And what we found

00:49:58.470 --> 00:50:01.350 align:middle line:84%
was that the behavior of
the volatility surface

00:50:01.350 --> 00:50:04.630 align:middle line:84%
was different in high and
low volatility environments.

00:50:04.630 --> 00:50:07.230 align:middle line:84%
In other words, if you
want to predict what's

00:50:07.230 --> 00:50:10.230 align:middle line:84%
going to happen to
the volatility surface

00:50:10.230 --> 00:50:13.710 align:middle line:84%
so you know how to change the
implied volatility when you

00:50:13.710 --> 00:50:17.607 align:middle line:84%
change the underlying
asset price,

00:50:17.607 --> 00:50:19.190 align:middle line:84%
you really want to
know whether you're

00:50:19.190 --> 00:50:22.690 align:middle line:84%
in a high volatility environment
or low volatility environment.

00:50:22.690 --> 00:50:28.590 align:middle line:84%
So this actually gave kind
of an interesting result,

00:50:28.590 --> 00:50:30.652 align:middle line:84%
which you'd be
unlikely to get just

00:50:30.652 --> 00:50:31.860 align:middle line:90%
playing around with the data.

00:50:31.860 --> 00:50:36.470 align:middle line:90%


00:50:36.470 --> 00:50:37.530 align:middle line:90%
Next slide, please.

00:50:37.530 --> 00:50:40.790 align:middle line:90%


00:50:40.790 --> 00:50:42.350 align:middle line:90%
We've got 20 minutes left.

00:50:42.350 --> 00:50:46.270 align:middle line:84%
So let's use the last 20 minutes
to talk about reinforcement

00:50:46.270 --> 00:50:50.350 align:middle line:84%
learning, which has
been a subject of quite

00:50:50.350 --> 00:50:53.530 align:middle line:84%
a lot of my work over
the last little while.

00:50:53.530 --> 00:50:55.890 align:middle line:90%
Next slide, please.

00:50:55.890 --> 00:50:57.450 align:middle line:84%
So what is
reinforcement learning?

00:50:57.450 --> 00:50:59.970 align:middle line:84%
It's concerned with
finding a strategy

00:50:59.970 --> 00:51:05.410 align:middle line:84%
for taking a series of
decisions, rather than just one.

00:51:05.410 --> 00:51:08.690 align:middle line:84%
So you're in some sort
of an environment.

00:51:08.690 --> 00:51:11.210 align:middle line:84%
It's changing in an
unpredictable way.

00:51:11.210 --> 00:51:12.700 align:middle line:90%
And you have to decide--

00:51:12.700 --> 00:51:15.295 align:middle line:90%


00:51:15.295 --> 00:51:17.170 align:middle line:84%
well, you have to decide
what decision you're

00:51:17.170 --> 00:51:19.290 align:middle line:84%
going to take
today, but you know

00:51:19.290 --> 00:51:22.090 align:middle line:84%
you're going to have to take
another decision tomorrow

00:51:22.090 --> 00:51:23.510 align:middle line:90%
and another the next day.

00:51:23.510 --> 00:51:26.210 align:middle line:84%
You know you're going to be
taking a series of decisions,

00:51:26.210 --> 00:51:29.290 align:middle line:90%
not a single one.

00:51:29.290 --> 00:51:34.730 align:middle line:84%
And this has actually
been a hugely successful--

00:51:34.730 --> 00:51:39.190 align:middle line:84%
it's kind of like a trial and
error kind of approach to this,

00:51:39.190 --> 00:51:40.970 align:middle line:90%
but it's been hugely successful.

00:51:40.970 --> 00:51:46.410 align:middle line:84%
I'm sure many of you
have heard of the success

00:51:46.410 --> 00:51:52.320 align:middle line:84%
that software has had in
playing games like chess and go.

00:51:52.320 --> 00:51:54.680 align:middle line:84%
It's actually we
developed software

00:51:54.680 --> 00:51:59.480 align:middle line:84%
that can beat the best human
players of chess and go.

00:51:59.480 --> 00:52:02.200 align:middle line:84%
I used to play a lot of
chess when I was younger.

00:52:02.200 --> 00:52:04.180 align:middle line:84%
And in a way, it's
disappointing.

00:52:04.180 --> 00:52:09.630 align:middle line:84%
But computers can now do this so
much better than human beings.

00:52:09.630 --> 00:52:12.720 align:middle line:90%


00:52:12.720 --> 00:52:14.600 align:middle line:90%
Next slide, please.

00:52:14.600 --> 00:52:21.680 align:middle line:84%
So the general model is you have
actions, states, and rewards.

00:52:21.680 --> 00:52:22.730 align:middle line:90%
So you take an action.

00:52:22.730 --> 00:52:26.960 align:middle line:90%


00:52:26.960 --> 00:52:29.880 align:middle line:84%
You start with a certain
state, which I call S0.

00:52:29.880 --> 00:52:33.560 align:middle line:84%
You take a certain
action at time 0.

00:52:33.560 --> 00:52:38.000 align:middle line:84%
And that moves
you to a new state

00:52:38.000 --> 00:52:39.680 align:middle line:90%
and maybe you get a reward.

00:52:39.680 --> 00:52:42.400 align:middle line:84%
Then you take
another action, which

00:52:42.400 --> 00:52:45.840 align:middle line:84%
will move you to
yet another state,

00:52:45.840 --> 00:52:50.160 align:middle line:84%
perhaps give you a
reward, and so on.

00:52:50.160 --> 00:52:55.740 align:middle line:84%
So you've got to look ahead and
think of all the actions you're

00:52:55.740 --> 00:53:01.500 align:middle line:84%
going to have to take, not just
at time 0, but at future times.

00:53:01.500 --> 00:53:07.260 align:middle line:84%
And the states at future
times are somewhat uncertain.

00:53:07.260 --> 00:53:08.320 align:middle line:90%
Next slide, please.

00:53:08.320 --> 00:53:11.540 align:middle line:90%


00:53:11.540 --> 00:53:13.300 align:middle line:90%
So one of the fundamental--

00:53:13.300 --> 00:53:16.740 align:middle line:84%
I mean, this is really a
trial and error process.

00:53:16.740 --> 00:53:22.420 align:middle line:84%
But if you-- and obviously,
you don't know anything

00:53:22.420 --> 00:53:23.780 align:middle line:90%
when you start.

00:53:23.780 --> 00:53:28.500 align:middle line:84%
So you're going to be choosing
some of a random strategy.

00:53:28.500 --> 00:53:31.700 align:middle line:84%
And then slowly,
over time, you learn

00:53:31.700 --> 00:53:34.580 align:middle line:84%
that doing this
in this situation

00:53:34.580 --> 00:53:38.060 align:middle line:84%
works out well, whereas doing
something else in that situation

00:53:38.060 --> 00:53:40.900 align:middle line:90%
works out badly.

00:53:40.900 --> 00:53:44.940 align:middle line:84%
And this is where exploration
versus exploitation comes in.

00:53:44.940 --> 00:53:48.500 align:middle line:84%
At any given time, there's
a certain probability

00:53:48.500 --> 00:53:50.830 align:middle line:84%
that you'll randomly
choose a decision.

00:53:50.830 --> 00:53:54.680 align:middle line:90%


00:53:54.680 --> 00:53:55.180 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:53:55.180 --> 00:53:57.545 align:middle line:84%
That's referred
to as exploration.

00:53:57.545 --> 00:53:58.920 align:middle line:84%
And there's a
certain probability

00:53:58.920 --> 00:54:03.560 align:middle line:84%
that you'll choose the decisions
that actually works out best so

00:54:03.560 --> 00:54:06.106 align:middle line:84%
far, which is
called exploitation.

00:54:06.106 --> 00:54:08.680 align:middle line:90%


00:54:08.680 --> 00:54:12.000 align:middle line:84%
So initially, the
probability of exploration

00:54:12.000 --> 00:54:14.040 align:middle line:90%
is 1 because you don't know it.

00:54:14.040 --> 00:54:17.320 align:middle line:84%
So you're going to
randomly choose a decision.

00:54:17.320 --> 00:54:20.640 align:middle line:84%
And then over
time, you'll slowly

00:54:20.640 --> 00:54:25.760 align:middle line:84%
reduce the probability
of exploration

00:54:25.760 --> 00:54:29.120 align:middle line:84%
and increase the
probability of exploitation.

00:54:29.120 --> 00:54:32.480 align:middle line:84%
And after a sufficient
number of trials,

00:54:32.480 --> 00:54:35.800 align:middle line:84%
you'll probably decide that,
actually, the probability

00:54:35.800 --> 00:54:38.423 align:middle line:90%
of exploitation should be one.

00:54:38.423 --> 00:54:39.840 align:middle line:84%
In other words,
you've pretty much

00:54:39.840 --> 00:54:46.280 align:middle line:84%
decided that you know
what the best decision is

00:54:46.280 --> 00:54:48.352 align:middle line:90%
in all different situations.

00:54:48.352 --> 00:54:51.700 align:middle line:90%


00:54:51.700 --> 00:54:54.163 align:middle line:90%
Next slide, please.

00:54:54.163 --> 00:54:55.580 align:middle line:84%
So I don't know
whether any of you

00:54:55.580 --> 00:54:58.500 align:middle line:90%
have come across this game Nim.

00:54:58.500 --> 00:54:59.000 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:54:59.000 --> 00:55:05.060 align:middle line:84%
But it's a very
simple game, but it's

00:55:05.060 --> 00:55:10.300 align:middle line:84%
a good game for illustrating how
reinforcement learning works.

00:55:10.300 --> 00:55:13.000 align:middle line:84%
So suppose we've
got two players.

00:55:13.000 --> 00:55:16.220 align:middle line:90%


00:55:16.220 --> 00:55:18.060 align:middle line:90%
So I'm playing against you.

00:55:18.060 --> 00:55:21.170 align:middle line:84%
And there's a pile of
matches on the table.

00:55:21.170 --> 00:55:23.780 align:middle line:90%


00:55:23.780 --> 00:55:26.860 align:middle line:90%
And we take turns.

00:55:26.860 --> 00:55:31.190 align:middle line:84%
Each of us can pick up
one, two, or three matches.

00:55:31.190 --> 00:55:34.740 align:middle line:90%


00:55:34.740 --> 00:55:38.260 align:middle line:84%
And you lose the game if you
have to pick up the last match.

00:55:38.260 --> 00:55:39.740 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:55:39.740 --> 00:55:41.780 align:middle line:90%
So pile of matches.

00:55:41.780 --> 00:55:44.560 align:middle line:84%
Take turns picking up one,
two, or three matches.

00:55:44.560 --> 00:55:49.920 align:middle line:84%
And the person who has to
pick up the last match loses.

00:55:49.920 --> 00:55:54.300 align:middle line:84%
Well, if you are being
good mathematicians,

00:55:54.300 --> 00:55:57.050 align:middle line:84%
you'd work out how to play
this game very quickly.

00:55:57.050 --> 00:56:01.280 align:middle line:90%


00:56:01.280 --> 00:56:07.240 align:middle line:84%
Basically, I mean, let's suppose
there are only five matches

00:56:07.240 --> 00:56:10.572 align:middle line:90%
on the table and it's your go.

00:56:10.572 --> 00:56:12.780 align:middle line:84%
There are five matches on
the table and it's your go.

00:56:12.780 --> 00:56:16.680 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to win because
if you pick up one match,

00:56:16.680 --> 00:56:18.060 align:middle line:90%
I'll pick up three matches.

00:56:18.060 --> 00:56:20.600 align:middle line:84%
And you have to pick
up the last match.

00:56:20.600 --> 00:56:26.000 align:middle line:84%
If you pick up two matches,
I'll pick up two matches

00:56:26.000 --> 00:56:29.520 align:middle line:84%
and you have to pick up
the last match and so on.

00:56:29.520 --> 00:56:34.200 align:middle line:84%
So if I can leave you with five
matches on the table, I win.

00:56:34.200 --> 00:56:36.500 align:middle line:84%
Then we can go one
stage beyond that.

00:56:36.500 --> 00:56:42.730 align:middle line:84%
Let's suppose I can leave you
with 9 matches on the table.

00:56:42.730 --> 00:56:44.480 align:middle line:84%
Then I can make sure
I leave you with five

00:56:44.480 --> 00:56:48.860 align:middle line:84%
matches next time,
because, again, there

00:56:48.860 --> 00:56:53.890 align:middle line:84%
are matches on the table, you
pick up one, I pick up three.

00:56:53.890 --> 00:56:56.860 align:middle line:90%


00:56:56.860 --> 00:57:00.900 align:middle line:84%
You're then left with
five matches on the table.

00:57:00.900 --> 00:57:04.120 align:middle line:84%
You pick up two, I pick up two,
you're left with five matches.

00:57:04.120 --> 00:57:07.840 align:middle line:84%
And very quickly you
decide that, actually,

00:57:07.840 --> 00:57:13.260 align:middle line:84%
the way to win this game is
to always leave your opponent,

00:57:13.260 --> 00:57:16.150 align:middle line:84%
if you can, with
4n plus 1 matches.

00:57:16.150 --> 00:57:19.624 align:middle line:90%


00:57:19.624 --> 00:57:25.580 align:middle line:84%
So I mean, you know,
suppose n is 5.

00:57:25.580 --> 00:57:27.540 align:middle line:90%
4n plus 1 is 21.

00:57:27.540 --> 00:57:31.020 align:middle line:84%
So if I can leave
you with 21 matches,

00:57:31.020 --> 00:57:35.700 align:middle line:84%
I will win because I'll
leave you with 21 matches.

00:57:35.700 --> 00:57:39.140 align:middle line:84%
Then whatever you
do next time round,

00:57:39.140 --> 00:57:42.380 align:middle line:90%
I'll leave you with 17 matches.

00:57:42.380 --> 00:57:44.290 align:middle line:84%
And then the next
time round around,

00:57:44.290 --> 00:57:49.730 align:middle line:84%
I'll leave you with
13 matches and so on.

00:57:49.730 --> 00:57:51.250 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:57:51.250 --> 00:57:57.890 align:middle line:84%
However, let's see how well
the machine does at working out

00:57:57.890 --> 00:58:00.130 align:middle line:90%
this best strategy.

00:58:00.130 --> 00:58:03.530 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to assume there's
only eight matches initially

00:58:03.530 --> 00:58:07.170 align:middle line:84%
on the table although
you'll find on my website,

00:58:07.170 --> 00:58:12.850 align:middle line:84%
you can find some software for
seeing how well the machine does

00:58:12.850 --> 00:58:14.770 align:middle line:90%
in a lot of other situations.

00:58:14.770 --> 00:58:20.910 align:middle line:84%
But we'll assume that the
reward is plus 1 from winning

00:58:20.910 --> 00:58:22.930 align:middle line:90%
and minus 1 from losing.

00:58:22.930 --> 00:58:26.810 align:middle line:84%
And your opponent
behaves randomly.

00:58:26.810 --> 00:58:30.610 align:middle line:84%
I can change things
so the opponent

00:58:30.610 --> 00:58:32.650 align:middle line:90%
learns as well if I want to.

00:58:32.650 --> 00:58:40.570 align:middle line:84%
So basically, we start with
this state action table.

00:58:40.570 --> 00:58:41.930 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:58:41.930 --> 00:58:45.850 align:middle line:84%
State is the number of
matches that are left, 1 to 8.

00:58:45.850 --> 00:58:48.630 align:middle line:84%
And the action is the
number I picked up.

00:58:48.630 --> 00:58:49.690 align:middle line:90%
Next slide, please.

00:58:49.690 --> 00:58:53.470 align:middle line:90%


00:58:53.470 --> 00:58:58.790 align:middle line:84%
So basically, as I said,
it's trial and error.

00:58:58.790 --> 00:59:00.230 align:middle line:90%
So let's suppose--

00:59:00.230 --> 00:59:02.430 align:middle line:90%
I mean you don't know anything.

00:59:02.430 --> 00:59:04.710 align:middle line:84%
So randomly, you choose
to pick up one match.

00:59:04.710 --> 00:59:07.430 align:middle line:84%
Randomly, your opponent
picks up three matches.

00:59:07.430 --> 00:59:09.750 align:middle line:84%
Randomly, you choose
to pick up one match.

00:59:09.750 --> 00:59:12.470 align:middle line:84%
Randomly, your opponent
picks up three matches.

00:59:12.470 --> 00:59:14.110 align:middle line:90%
You win.

00:59:14.110 --> 00:59:15.590 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:59:15.590 --> 00:59:22.710 align:middle line:84%
So we can update Q8,1, 8
being the number of matches

00:59:22.710 --> 00:59:26.010 align:middle line:84%
on the table and the 1
being the number picked up.

00:59:26.010 --> 00:59:29.330 align:middle line:84%
So the first argument is the
number of matches on the table.

00:59:29.330 --> 00:59:30.990 align:middle line:90%
Second is the number picked up.

00:59:30.990 --> 00:59:38.990 align:middle line:84%
So we know from that that we
have a trial where we win.

00:59:38.990 --> 00:59:45.090 align:middle line:84%
Q8,1 leads to a win and
Q4,1 leads to a win.

00:59:45.090 --> 00:59:53.010 align:middle line:90%
So we update Q8,1 and Q4,1.

00:59:53.010 --> 00:59:54.674 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:59:54.674 --> 00:59:58.196 align:middle line:84%
And we have to have an updating
parameter of the formula.

00:59:58.196 --> 01:00:02.910 align:middle line:90%
Formula for updating is there.

01:00:02.910 --> 01:00:07.370 align:middle line:84%
And we say, well, OK, we
don't want to update too much.

01:00:07.370 --> 01:00:11.250 align:middle line:90%
We'll give it 5% weight.

01:00:11.250 --> 01:00:21.250 align:middle line:84%
So we have the old Q SA plus 5%
of the gain minus the old Q SA.

01:00:21.250 --> 01:00:25.190 align:middle line:84%
And can we have the
next slide, please?

01:00:25.190 --> 01:00:29.170 align:middle line:90%


01:00:29.170 --> 01:00:33.250 align:middle line:84%
On each trial, we observe
certain states take action

01:00:33.250 --> 01:00:34.730 align:middle line:90%
in those states.

01:00:34.730 --> 01:00:38.970 align:middle line:84%
And the gain used
in updating the Qs

01:00:38.970 --> 01:00:45.230 align:middle line:84%
can be either the final reward,
referred to as the Monte Carlo

01:00:45.230 --> 01:00:49.350 align:middle line:84%
method, or it can be the
value of the next state,

01:00:49.350 --> 01:00:52.030 align:middle line:84%
assuming the best
decision is taken.

01:00:52.030 --> 01:00:59.510 align:middle line:84%
So if I pick up one
match, and you pick up

01:00:59.510 --> 01:01:02.590 align:middle line:90%
one match, then the value--

01:01:02.590 --> 01:01:04.630 align:middle line:84%
and there are six
matches on the table,

01:01:04.630 --> 01:01:07.710 align:middle line:84%
and in terms of updating,
instead of waiting

01:01:07.710 --> 01:01:10.090 align:middle line:84%
to see whether I win or
lose, I could just say,

01:01:10.090 --> 01:01:12.670 align:middle line:84%
how good is it for me if
there are just six matches

01:01:12.670 --> 01:01:13.910 align:middle line:90%
left on the table?

01:01:13.910 --> 01:01:18.150 align:middle line:84%
And that would be the way
we would do the updating.

01:01:18.150 --> 01:01:20.150 align:middle line:84%
So we start with the
Qs all 0, and then

01:01:20.150 --> 01:01:24.590 align:middle line:84%
we update them in the way
that I was describing.

01:01:24.590 --> 01:01:25.730 align:middle line:90%
Next slide, please.

01:01:25.730 --> 01:01:29.830 align:middle line:90%


01:01:29.830 --> 01:01:32.590 align:middle line:84%
And with the Monte
Carlo method, which

01:01:32.590 --> 01:01:36.150 align:middle line:84%
is where we wait and see whether
we win or not, you can see--

01:01:36.150 --> 01:01:40.390 align:middle line:84%
I mean, we all know that with
eight matches on the table,

01:01:40.390 --> 01:01:44.050 align:middle line:84%
if it's our go, we pick up
three and leave our opponent

01:01:44.050 --> 01:01:45.610 align:middle line:90%
with five matches on the table.

01:01:45.610 --> 01:01:47.690 align:middle line:90%
That's the best decision.

01:01:47.690 --> 01:01:52.910 align:middle line:84%
After 1,000 trials, it hasn't
really found the best decision.

01:01:52.910 --> 01:01:55.050 align:middle line:84%
It still looks as though
picking up one match

01:01:55.050 --> 01:01:57.770 align:middle line:90%
is marginally better.

01:01:57.770 --> 01:02:03.570 align:middle line:84%
The Q value is 0.562
as opposed to 0.522.

01:02:03.570 --> 01:02:06.770 align:middle line:84%
After 5,000 trials, it's
definitely beginning to look

01:02:06.770 --> 01:02:10.530 align:middle line:84%
that it's best to pick up three
matches and after 10,000 trials

01:02:10.530 --> 01:02:12.930 align:middle line:90%
for sure.

01:02:12.930 --> 01:02:14.470 align:middle line:84%
So you see what's
happening here.

01:02:14.470 --> 01:02:16.970 align:middle line:84%
We're just doing
more and more trials.

01:02:16.970 --> 01:02:24.850 align:middle line:84%
And what we did here was we had
this epsilon parameter which

01:02:24.850 --> 01:02:29.730 align:middle line:84%
determines whether
you behave randomly

01:02:29.730 --> 01:02:33.330 align:middle line:84%
or you use the best
decision that seems

01:02:33.330 --> 01:02:35.330 align:middle line:90%
to have worked out so far.

01:02:35.330 --> 01:02:37.750 align:middle line:84%
We have epsilon
starting at 1 and having

01:02:37.750 --> 01:02:43.230 align:middle line:90%
this decay factor of 0.9995.

01:02:43.230 --> 01:02:46.910 align:middle line:84%
So it does appear that
reinforcement learning

01:02:46.910 --> 01:02:49.270 align:middle line:90%
finds the best decision.

01:02:49.270 --> 01:02:51.990 align:middle line:90%
Next slide, please.

01:02:51.990 --> 01:02:54.590 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, it finds
it even more quickly

01:02:54.590 --> 01:02:56.050 align:middle line:84%
with temporal
difference learning.

01:02:56.050 --> 01:03:03.390 align:middle line:84%
This is where you look to
see how much does it turn out

01:03:03.390 --> 01:03:05.910 align:middle line:84%
to be worth to be in
the next situation

01:03:05.910 --> 01:03:08.750 align:middle line:84%
that you're in after you've
taken the first decision?

01:03:08.750 --> 01:03:12.190 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that,
as often is the case,

01:03:12.190 --> 01:03:16.070 align:middle line:84%
temporal difference learning
works better than the raw Monte

01:03:16.070 --> 01:03:19.230 align:middle line:90%
Carlo simulation.

01:03:19.230 --> 01:03:27.790 align:middle line:84%
But it's definitely
worked out that it's best

01:03:27.790 --> 01:03:32.230 align:middle line:90%
to pick up three matches.

01:03:32.230 --> 01:03:34.952 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't look so bad to pick
up two matches or one match

01:03:34.952 --> 01:03:37.160 align:middle line:84%
because, remember, your
opponent's behaving randomly.

01:03:37.160 --> 01:03:40.140 align:middle line:90%


01:03:40.140 --> 01:03:43.260 align:middle line:84%
And when you've learnt a lot,
you can behave much better than

01:03:43.260 --> 01:03:45.860 align:middle line:90%
your opponent.

01:03:45.860 --> 01:03:49.500 align:middle line:84%
I've done other tests where
the opponent is learning

01:03:49.500 --> 01:03:55.060 align:middle line:84%
as well, in which case picking
up two matches or one match

01:03:55.060 --> 01:03:59.700 align:middle line:84%
is going to allow
your opponent to win.

01:03:59.700 --> 01:04:04.160 align:middle line:84%
And so you have negative numbers
opposite the 2 and the 1.

01:04:04.160 --> 01:04:07.660 align:middle line:90%


01:04:07.660 --> 01:04:10.400 align:middle line:90%
Next slide, please.

01:04:10.400 --> 01:04:17.570 align:middle line:90%


01:04:17.570 --> 01:04:23.300 align:middle line:84%
This Nim game
illustrates, admittedly

01:04:23.300 --> 01:04:25.460 align:middle line:84%
in a fairly simple
situation, how

01:04:25.460 --> 01:04:28.460 align:middle line:90%
reinforcement learning works.

01:04:28.460 --> 01:04:36.280 align:middle line:84%
Very often you've got a much
more complex state action table

01:04:36.280 --> 01:04:38.560 align:middle line:90%
to be filled in.

01:04:38.560 --> 01:04:43.680 align:middle line:90%


01:04:43.680 --> 01:04:49.043 align:middle line:84%
You have to combine
reinforcement learning

01:04:49.043 --> 01:04:50.960 align:middle line:84%
with artificial neural
networks because you've

01:04:50.960 --> 01:04:53.320 align:middle line:90%
got this state action table.

01:04:53.320 --> 01:05:00.120 align:middle line:84%
You're able to fill in some of
the numbers in the state action

01:05:00.120 --> 01:05:03.080 align:middle line:90%
table but not all of them.

01:05:03.080 --> 01:05:06.780 align:middle line:84%
But you know there's some
general non-linear function

01:05:06.780 --> 01:05:09.840 align:middle line:84%
which is describing
the whole table.

01:05:09.840 --> 01:05:12.600 align:middle line:84%
And you have to do the best
you can with the numbers

01:05:12.600 --> 01:05:15.800 align:middle line:90%
that you do have on the table.

01:05:15.800 --> 01:05:19.060 align:middle line:90%
Next slide, please.

01:05:19.060 --> 01:05:19.560 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:05:19.560 --> 01:05:23.440 align:middle line:90%
So five minutes left.

01:05:23.440 --> 01:05:27.200 align:middle line:84%
What I've been spending
a lot of my time

01:05:27.200 --> 01:05:32.120 align:middle line:84%
in the last little
while on is saying,

01:05:32.120 --> 01:05:34.500 align:middle line:84%
can we use
reinforcement learning

01:05:34.500 --> 01:05:36.160 align:middle line:90%
for hedging derivatives?

01:05:36.160 --> 01:05:38.900 align:middle line:90%


01:05:38.900 --> 01:05:40.540 align:middle line:84%
The traditional
approach to hedging

01:05:40.540 --> 01:05:43.300 align:middle line:84%
is, of course, Greek
letters where you calculate

01:05:43.300 --> 01:05:46.140 align:middle line:90%
delta, gamma, vega and so on.

01:05:46.140 --> 01:05:50.300 align:middle line:84%
And you're really looking at
a very short period of time

01:05:50.300 --> 01:05:55.620 align:middle line:84%
ahead because
you're saying what's

01:05:55.620 --> 01:06:00.340 align:middle line:84%
the partial derivative of
the value of my portfolio

01:06:00.340 --> 01:06:05.220 align:middle line:84%
with respect to whatever
it is, the underlying asset

01:06:05.220 --> 01:06:10.940 align:middle line:90%
price, volatility, or whatever.

01:06:10.940 --> 01:06:14.240 align:middle line:84%
Reinforcement learning
has a different approach.

01:06:14.240 --> 01:06:19.220 align:middle line:84%
It says let's look several
periods ahead and try and take

01:06:19.220 --> 01:06:28.700 align:middle line:84%
a decision which will work
well over our time horizon.

01:06:28.700 --> 01:06:29.670 align:middle line:90%
So what have we found?

01:06:29.670 --> 01:06:32.300 align:middle line:90%


01:06:32.300 --> 01:06:32.910 align:middle line:90%
Next slide.

01:06:32.910 --> 01:06:37.000 align:middle line:90%


01:06:37.000 --> 01:06:38.800 align:middle line:90%
This is my last slide.

01:06:38.800 --> 01:06:44.640 align:middle line:84%
So what we found is we found
that, in some situations,

01:06:44.640 --> 01:06:47.620 align:middle line:84%
using reinforcement learning
for hedging-- in other words,

01:06:47.620 --> 01:06:50.920 align:middle line:84%
looking several periods ahead
and seeing which strategies will

01:06:50.920 --> 01:06:53.944 align:middle line:90%
work out best--

01:06:53.944 --> 01:06:55.720 align:middle line:90%
can be useful.

01:06:55.720 --> 01:07:03.320 align:middle line:84%
For vanilla options,
it does about the--

01:07:03.320 --> 01:07:09.120 align:middle line:84%
overall, it does about the same
as the Greek letter hedging.

01:07:09.120 --> 01:07:13.200 align:middle line:84%
But it does save
transaction costs

01:07:13.200 --> 01:07:15.920 align:middle line:84%
and actually saves significant
amounts of transaction costs

01:07:15.920 --> 01:07:17.587 align:middle line:84%
because you can think
about it this way.

01:07:17.587 --> 01:07:20.680 align:middle line:90%
You're hedging a vanilla option.

01:07:20.680 --> 01:07:23.280 align:middle line:84%
I mean let's just talk
in terms of delta.

01:07:23.280 --> 01:07:28.440 align:middle line:84%
I mean, let's suppose
delta is 0.5 right now.

01:07:28.440 --> 01:07:34.140 align:middle line:90%
And delta goes up to 0.7.

01:07:34.140 --> 01:07:38.560 align:middle line:84%
But maybe there's a 50% chance
that your next hedging date,

01:07:38.560 --> 01:07:42.580 align:middle line:90%
delta will go back down to 0.5.

01:07:42.580 --> 01:07:47.850 align:middle line:84%
So the question is,
should you change?

01:07:47.850 --> 01:07:50.380 align:middle line:90%


01:07:50.380 --> 01:07:53.180 align:middle line:84%
Should you do your
hedging to reflect

01:07:53.180 --> 01:07:56.580 align:middle line:84%
delta going from 0.5
to 0.7 even though you

01:07:56.580 --> 01:07:59.760 align:middle line:84%
know there's a 50% chance
it'll go back down to 0.5

01:07:59.760 --> 01:08:04.460 align:middle line:84%
or is it better to wait
and see what happens

01:08:04.460 --> 01:08:06.610 align:middle line:90%
over perhaps two time periods?

01:08:06.610 --> 01:08:09.620 align:middle line:90%


01:08:09.620 --> 01:08:13.860 align:middle line:84%
And the answer is if there's
a lot of transaction costs,

01:08:13.860 --> 01:08:15.500 align:middle line:90%
it's better to wait.

01:08:15.500 --> 01:08:17.540 align:middle line:84%
So we find
reinforcement learning

01:08:17.540 --> 01:08:22.020 align:middle line:84%
can save quite a lot of
transaction costs even

01:08:22.020 --> 01:08:25.979 align:middle line:84%
though, when you don't take
account of transaction costs,

01:08:25.979 --> 01:08:30.290 align:middle line:84%
there's not a huge saving for
vanilla options, typically.

01:08:30.290 --> 01:08:33.490 align:middle line:84%
And of course,
transaction costs are

01:08:33.490 --> 01:08:36.729 align:middle line:84%
really important when it comes
to vega and gamma hedging

01:08:36.729 --> 01:08:39.450 align:middle line:90%
as well.

01:08:39.450 --> 01:08:42.930 align:middle line:84%
But some exciting options
such as barrier options

01:08:42.930 --> 01:08:47.830 align:middle line:84%
we find reinforcement learning
produces superior results,

01:08:47.830 --> 01:08:49.660 align:middle line:84%
even when there are
no transaction costs.

01:08:49.660 --> 01:08:52.170 align:middle line:90%


01:08:52.170 --> 01:08:55.490 align:middle line:84%
And one of the nice things about
using reinforcement learning

01:08:55.490 --> 01:08:59.410 align:middle line:84%
is that you can choose
your objective function

01:08:59.410 --> 01:09:04.529 align:middle line:84%
whereas, of course, you can't do
that with Greek letter hedging.

01:09:04.529 --> 01:09:13.370 align:middle line:84%
So we've experimented
with looking

01:09:13.370 --> 01:09:15.569 align:middle line:90%
at the tail of the distribution.

01:09:15.569 --> 01:09:21.490 align:middle line:84%
And VaR95 is a shorthand for you
want the 95th percentile point

01:09:21.490 --> 01:09:27.210 align:middle line:84%
of the loss distribution
to be as high as possible.

01:09:27.210 --> 01:09:32.090 align:middle line:84%
Or CVaR95 is where you want
the conditional value at risk,

01:09:32.090 --> 01:09:39.189 align:middle line:84%
which is the average beyond
the 95th percentile of the loss

01:09:39.189 --> 01:09:42.790 align:middle line:84%
distribution to be
as low as possible.

01:09:42.790 --> 01:09:45.270 align:middle line:84%
So you can have an
objective function

01:09:45.270 --> 01:09:54.870 align:middle line:84%
that focuses on the things
you're most concerned about,

01:09:54.870 --> 01:09:57.710 align:middle line:84%
which is that perhaps the
tail of the distribution.

01:09:57.710 --> 01:10:01.750 align:middle line:84%
We find it's robust, gives good
results during stress periods.

01:10:01.750 --> 01:10:02.670 align:middle line:90%
And it's interesting.

01:10:02.670 --> 01:10:07.790 align:middle line:84%
JP Morgan is our main
competitor in terms of doing

01:10:07.790 --> 01:10:09.650 align:middle line:90%
this research is JP Morgan.

01:10:09.650 --> 01:10:14.910 align:middle line:84%
JP Morgan's been doing a lot of
work looking at reinforcement

01:10:14.910 --> 01:10:17.430 align:middle line:90%
learning for hedging.

01:10:17.430 --> 01:10:21.030 align:middle line:84%
Of course, the disadvantage
of reinforcement learning

01:10:21.030 --> 01:10:25.910 align:middle line:84%
is that it's much more
computationally demanding

01:10:25.910 --> 01:10:27.170 align:middle line:90%
than traditional approaches.

01:10:27.170 --> 01:10:31.370 align:middle line:84%
You've got to run
these trial and error

01:10:31.370 --> 01:10:36.290 align:middle line:84%
programs, which can
take a few minutes,

01:10:36.290 --> 01:10:38.930 align:middle line:90%
but they're getting faster.

01:10:38.930 --> 01:10:42.370 align:middle line:84%
So whereas it takes no time at
all to calculate a Greek letter,

01:10:42.370 --> 01:10:45.730 align:middle line:84%
it will take you a little
while to calculate the best

01:10:45.730 --> 01:10:50.930 align:middle line:84%
strategy for doing
reinforcement learning hedging.

01:10:50.930 --> 01:10:54.370 align:middle line:84%
But I think, to come
back to my earlier point

01:10:54.370 --> 01:11:02.770 align:middle line:84%
that computers are now
sufficiently fast that it's

01:11:02.770 --> 01:11:06.290 align:middle line:90%
becoming a feasible tool.

01:11:06.290 --> 01:11:11.962 align:middle line:90%
So brings me to my last slide.

01:11:11.962 --> 01:11:14.470 align:middle line:90%
We're one minute over 4:00 mark.

01:11:14.470 --> 01:11:16.750 align:middle line:84%
But if anybody wants
to ask any questions,

01:11:16.750 --> 01:11:20.138 align:middle line:90%
I'd be happy to answer them.

01:11:20.138 --> 01:11:21.930 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Well, I have
a question for you,

01:11:21.930 --> 01:11:28.350 align:middle line:84%
John, about the choice
of activation functions.

01:11:28.350 --> 01:11:31.530 align:middle line:84%
Have you found, as
you did in this case,

01:11:31.530 --> 01:11:34.790 align:middle line:84%
that I guess it was
the sigmoid function

01:11:34.790 --> 01:11:42.750 align:middle line:84%
was applied in your
example or was it the ReLU?

01:11:42.750 --> 01:11:43.990 align:middle line:90%
JOHN HULL: PROFESSOR: Yeah.

01:11:43.990 --> 01:11:49.430 align:middle line:84%
We've had a lot of success
with the sigmoid function.

01:11:49.430 --> 01:11:53.670 align:middle line:84%
That may be just
chance events or maybe

01:11:53.670 --> 01:11:59.733 align:middle line:84%
there's something special
about the sigmoid function.

01:11:59.733 --> 01:12:01.150 align:middle line:84%
I mean, of course,
there's nothing

01:12:01.150 --> 01:12:05.350 align:middle line:84%
to stop you trying several
different activation functions

01:12:05.350 --> 01:12:11.030 align:middle line:84%
and seeing which works out
best in a particular situation.

01:12:11.030 --> 01:12:20.030 align:middle line:84%
And we did that in our
implied volatility research.

01:12:20.030 --> 01:12:22.230 align:middle line:84%
And we concluded that
sigmoid function works worked

01:12:22.230 --> 01:12:24.050 align:middle line:90%
out best as a matter of fact.

01:12:24.050 --> 01:12:27.510 align:middle line:84%
But we tried several different
activation functions.

01:12:27.510 --> 01:12:34.790 align:middle line:90%


01:12:34.790 --> 01:12:37.290 align:middle line:84%
But I think, if I
remember correctly,

01:12:37.290 --> 01:12:41.050 align:middle line:84%
all the activation
functions we tried

01:12:41.050 --> 01:12:44.850 align:middle line:84%
were pretty close to
each other in terms

01:12:44.850 --> 01:12:46.430 align:middle line:90%
of how well they worked out.

01:12:46.430 --> 01:12:49.850 align:middle line:90%


01:12:49.850 --> 01:12:54.010 align:middle line:84%
So I think choosing the
activation function may not be,

01:12:54.010 --> 01:12:59.330 align:middle line:84%
in many situations anyway,
may not be a big deal.

01:12:59.330 --> 01:13:05.050 align:middle line:84%
What you also have to
think is how big do you

01:13:05.050 --> 01:13:10.690 align:middle line:84%
want the models to be,
how many neurons per step,

01:13:10.690 --> 01:13:15.490 align:middle line:84%
how many steps in total,
and that sort of thing.

01:13:15.490 --> 01:13:20.910 align:middle line:84%
I think that's very
typically what happens.

01:13:20.910 --> 01:13:22.410 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Is there
a way to benefit

01:13:22.410 --> 01:13:27.490 align:middle line:84%
from building the neural
network with, say, two layers

01:13:27.490 --> 01:13:31.810 align:middle line:84%
and then use the results of
that to add a third layer,

01:13:31.810 --> 01:13:36.590 align:middle line:84%
or is the three layer
neural network just built

01:13:36.590 --> 01:13:37.290 align:middle line:90%
independently?

01:13:37.290 --> 01:13:38.830 align:middle line:84%
JOHN HULL: That's
a good question.

01:13:38.830 --> 01:13:42.117 align:middle line:84%
As far as I know, you've
got to start from scratch

01:13:42.117 --> 01:13:44.200 align:middle line:84%
when you go from, say, two
layers to three layers.

01:13:44.200 --> 01:13:48.710 align:middle line:90%


01:13:48.710 --> 01:13:54.230 align:middle line:84%
You can't use what you've done
with two layers as a shortcut

01:13:54.230 --> 01:13:55.260 align:middle line:90%
to doing three layers.

01:13:55.260 --> 01:13:58.070 align:middle line:90%


01:13:58.070 --> 01:14:00.830 align:middle line:84%
I may be wrong there, but I
don't think there's any way.

01:14:00.830 --> 01:14:03.350 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Right.

01:14:03.350 --> 01:14:05.630 align:middle line:84%
Actually, for the
class's benefit,

01:14:05.630 --> 01:14:09.590 align:middle line:90%
I checked your references.

01:14:09.590 --> 01:14:14.710 align:middle line:84%
And on your Toronto
website, you actually

01:14:14.710 --> 01:14:19.430 align:middle line:84%
have material like the Python
code for various examples

01:14:19.430 --> 01:14:20.210 align:middle line:90%
in your book.

01:14:20.210 --> 01:14:25.340 align:middle line:84%
So I think students might find
that very accessible and useful.

01:14:25.340 --> 01:14:26.500 align:middle line:90%
JOHN HULL: Yeah.

01:14:26.500 --> 01:14:27.632 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:14:27.632 --> 01:14:28.840 align:middle line:90%
I should have mentioned this.

01:14:28.840 --> 01:14:29.340 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:14:29.340 --> 01:14:30.820 align:middle line:90%
There's a lot on my website.

01:14:30.820 --> 01:14:35.740 align:middle line:84%
I think lots of
different things we've

01:14:35.740 --> 01:14:43.660 align:middle line:84%
done at different times on
machine learning research.

01:14:43.660 --> 01:14:48.380 align:middle line:84%
So if you want to
read more about it,

01:14:48.380 --> 01:14:52.340 align:middle line:84%
certainly my website would
be a good place to go.

01:14:52.340 --> 01:14:56.780 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: How do you choose
the objective functions

01:14:56.780 --> 01:14:59.020 align:middle line:90%
in your examples?

01:14:59.020 --> 01:15:04.540 align:middle line:84%
Was there some issues
involved with choosing those

01:15:04.540 --> 01:15:05.460 align:middle line:90%
specifically?

01:15:05.460 --> 01:15:08.437 align:middle line:90%


01:15:08.437 --> 01:15:10.520 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: And what was the
difference between them?

01:15:10.520 --> 01:15:13.333 align:middle line:90%


01:15:13.333 --> 01:15:14.000 align:middle line:90%
JOHN HULL: Yeah.

01:15:14.000 --> 01:15:18.700 align:middle line:84%
I think every
situation is different

01:15:18.700 --> 01:15:20.200 align:middle line:90%
as far as that's concerned.

01:15:20.200 --> 01:15:24.160 align:middle line:90%


01:15:24.160 --> 01:15:29.160 align:middle line:84%
You've got to think what
objective function fits in

01:15:29.160 --> 01:15:37.240 align:middle line:84%
with what I'm trying to do
in the particular situation

01:15:37.240 --> 01:15:38.500 align:middle line:90%
that you're working on.

01:15:38.500 --> 01:15:39.000 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:15:39.000 --> 01:15:42.200 align:middle line:90%


01:15:42.200 --> 01:15:44.840 align:middle line:84%
I mean, if you've got
some exotic option

01:15:44.840 --> 01:15:49.200 align:middle line:84%
and you're trying to match the
price as closely as possible,

01:15:49.200 --> 01:15:51.520 align:middle line:84%
then, obviously,
it's the difference

01:15:51.520 --> 01:15:56.240 align:middle line:84%
between the price
you're trying to match

01:15:56.240 --> 01:16:00.920 align:middle line:84%
and the price given
by the neural network

01:16:00.920 --> 01:16:03.896 align:middle line:90%
is going to be most--

01:16:03.896 --> 01:16:07.730 align:middle line:84%
there's going to be an objective
that you're trying to minimize.

01:16:07.730 --> 01:16:21.000 align:middle line:90%